window.CET6_BANKS.push({"id": "2021-06-2", "source": "2021年06月真题·第2套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay based on the chart below. You should start your essay with a brief description of the chart and comment on China's achievements in higher education . You should write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words.", "sample": "The line chart illustrates the changes in the gross enrolment ratio in higher education in China from 1990 to\n2019. As is clearly shown, the figure witnessed a steady and remarkable upward trend over these three decades.\nIn 1990, only 3.4% of the eligible population entered higher education. This proportion climbed steadily,\npassing 11% in 2000 and 30% in 2012, before finally reaching 51.6% in 2019, roughly fifteen times the\ninitial level.\nBehind these figures lie China's impressive achievements in higher education. First, the dramatic rise reflects\nthe massive expansion of university enrolment, which has opened the door of college to millions of ordinary\nfamilies. Moreover, the higher enrolment ratio means a far larger pool of skilled talent, supplying the economy\nwith engineers, doctors and researchers. In my view, such progress not only improves individual prospects but\nalso strengthens the nation's competitiveness.\nIn conclusion, the steady climb of the enrolment ratio speaks volumes about how far Chinese higher education\nhas advanced, and I firmly believe this positive momentum will continue in the years ahead.", "image": "img/2021-06-2.jpg"}, "translation": {"prompt": "青海是中国西北部的一个省份，平均海拔3000米以上，大部分地区为高山和高原。青海省得名于全国最大的咸水湖青海湖。青海湖被誉为\"中国最美的湖泊\"，是最受欢迎的旅游景点之一，也是摄影师和艺术家的天堂。 青海山川壮丽，地大物博，石油和天然气储量丰富，省内许多城市的经济在石油和天然气工业带动下得到了长足发展。青海尤以水资源丰富而闻名，是中国三大河流长江、黄河和澜沧江的发源地，在中国的水生态中发挥着重要作用。", "sample": "Qinghai is a province in northwest China, with an average elevation of over 3,000 meters, and most of its area is covered by mountains and plateaus. The province is named after Qinghai Lake, the largest saltwater lake in China. Qinghai Lake is praised as \"the most beautiful lake in China\". It is one of the most popular tourist attractions and a paradise for photographers and artists. Qinghai is blessed with magnificent mountains and rivers, a vast territory rich in natural resources, and abundant reserves of oil and natural gas. Driven by the oil and natural gas industry, the economy of many cities in the province has developed rapidly. Qinghai is especially famous for its abundant water resources. It is the source of China's three major rivers, namely the Yangtze, the Yellow River and the Lancang River, playing an important role in China's water ecology."}, "listeningUrl": "https://english-exam.lazynote.cn/cet6/paper/2021-06-2/?f=w", "clozePassage": "I'm always baffled when I walk into a pharmacy and see shelves bursting with various vitamins, extracts and other supplements, all promising to accelerate or promote weight loss. Aisles of marketing genius belie(掩饰) the fact that,  __26__  , weight loss is dictated by the laws of arithmetic. Economist Jessica Irvine wrote a book about how she used math to help her lose more than 18 kilograms. If calories taken in are less than calories  __27__  , weight shall be lost, and so it is with money.\nDespite the  __28__  of financial products, services and solutions geared towards accumulating wealth, it all begins with the same  __29__  : getting ahead financially requires a reduction of spending, so that income is greater than expenses. I was reminded of this again recently listening to an interview with Nicole Haddow, the author of Smashed Avocado, explaining how she cracked the property market at 31. It was quite a  __30__  , given where she had been two years earlier.\nNicole didn't celebrate her 30th birthday as she had  __31__  . She was sobbing at the dinner table with her parents, with whom she had just moved back in. She had no stable income, $12,000 in credit-card debt and no plan, but to her  __32__  , her father, an accountant, told her that her financial  __33__  wasn't as bad as she thought. He said, on her income, with some changes, she would be able to buy an investment unit within two years, which she did.\nNicole admitted she was fortunate, as she was able to live with her parents and  __34__  her spending—and life—to get herself on track financially. Creating a gap between her income and spending required a paradigm shift and  __35__  sacrifice and commitment, but by going into financial lockdown, Nicole gained financial independence.", "matchIntro": "France's beloved cathedral only minutes away from complete destruction", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "Notre Dame Cathedral in the heart of Paris was within \"15 to 30 minutes\" of complete destruction as firefighters battled to stop flames reaching its bell towers on Monday evening, French authorities have revealed. A greater disaster was averted by members of the Paris fire brigade, who risked their lives to remain inside the burning monument to create a wall of water between the raging fire and the two towers on the west of the building."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The revelation of how close France came to losing its most famous cathedral emerged as police investigators questioned workers involved in the restoration of the monument to try to establish the cause of the devastating blaze. Paris prosecutor Remy Heitz said that an initial fire alert was sounded at 6:20 pm on Monday evening but no fire was found. The second alert was sounded at 6:43 pm, and the blaze was discovered on the roof."}, {"label": "C", "text": "More than €650 million was raised in a few hours on Tuesday as French business leaders and global corporations announced they would donate to a restoration campaign launched by the president, Emmanuel Macron. But as the emergency services picked through the burnt debris, a row was resurfacing over accusations that the beloved cathedral, immortalised in Victor Hugo's novel, was already crumbling before the fire."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The cathedral is owned by the French state and has been at the centre of a years-long dispute over who should finance restoration work of the collapsing staircases, crumbling statues and cracked walls. Jean-Michel Leniaud, the president of the scientific council at the National Heritage Institute, said: \"What happened was bound to happen. The lack of adequate maintenance and daily attention to such a majestic building is the cause of this catastrophe.\" After the blaze was declared completely extinguished, 15 hours after it started, the junior interior minister, Laurent Nunez, said the structure had been saved but remained vulnerable. He praised the actions of the firefighters but admitted the fate of the cathedral had been uncertain. \"They saved the main structure, but it all came down to 15-30 minutes,\" Nunez said."}, {"label": "E", "text": "In a surprise televised address on Tuesday evening, Macron said he wanted to see the cathedral rebuilt within five years. \"The fire at Notre Dame reminds us that we will always have challenges to overcome,\" Macron said, \"Notre Dame is our history, our literature, the centre of our life. It is the standard by which we measure our distances. It's so many books, so many paintings. It's the cathedral of every French person, even those who have never visited it. This history is ours and so we will rebuild Notre Dame. It is what the French people expect; it is what our history deserves. It is our deep destiny. We will rebuild Notre Dame so it is even more beautiful than before. I want it done in the next five years. We can do it. After the time of testing comes a time of reflection and then of action.\""}, {"label": "F", "text": "The fire, which had started at the base of the 93-metre spire(尖塔) at about 6:40 pm on Monday, spread through the cathedral's roof, made up of hundreds of oak beams, some dating back to the 13th century. These beams, known as la forêt (the forest) because of their density, formed the cross-shaped roof that ran the length of the central part of the cathedral. As hundreds of tourists and Parisians stood and watched the flames leaping from the roof, there was shock and tears as the cathedral spire caught fire, burned and then collapsed into itself."}, {"label": "G", "text": "A collection of dramatic videos and photos quickly spread across social media, showing the horrifying destruction, and attracting emotional responses from people all over the world. Indeed, within minutes the fire occupied headlines of every major global newspaper and television network. This is not surprising given Notre Dame Cathedral, meaning \"Our Lady\", is one of the most recognised symbols of the city of Paris attracting millions of tourists every year."}, {"label": "H", "text": "While the world looked on, the 500 firefighters at the scene then battled to prevent the flames from reaching the two main towers, where the cathedral bells hang. If the wooden frame of the towers had caught fire, it could have sent the bells—the largest of which, the Emmanuel Bell, weighs 13 tons—crashing down, potentially causing the collapse of both towers. Police and fire services will spend the next 48 hours assessing the \"security and safety\" of the 850-year-old structure. Nunez said: \"We have identified vulnerabilities throughout the structure, all of which still need securing.\" As a result, residents of five buildings around the northern side of the cathedral were being temporarily evacuated, he added. Architects have identified three main holes in the structure, in the locations of the spire, the main hall and the upper rooms to the north of the central aisle. Most of the wooden roof beams have been burned, and parts of the concrete holding up the roof have collapsed."}, {"label": "I", "text": "The interior minister, Christophe Castaner, visited the cathedral on Tuesday afternoon to see the extent of the devastation. Ash covered the marble diamond-patterned floor and floated in large pools of grey water from the fire hoses. Behind a heap of blackened oak beams that lay piled up where they had fallen, daylight from vast holes in the cathedral roof lit a golden cross over a statue by Nicolas Coustou, which appeared to have escaped damage. Preliminary inspections also suggested the three ornate(装饰华丽的) stained glass \"rose\" windows appeared to have survived the fire, officials said. However, fire officers have said a complete inventory of the damage will not be possible until the cathedral structure has been deemed safe."}, {"label": "J", "text": "The culture minister, Franck Riester, said religious relics saved from the cathedral were being securely held at the Hotel de Ville, and works of art that sustained smoke damage were being taken to the Louvre, the world's largest art museum, where they would be dried out, repaired and stored. Sixteen copper statues that decorated the spire had been removed for restoration only a few days before the fire. Relics at the top of the spire are believed lost as the spire was destroyed. As well as damage from the heat, which firefighters said reached more than 800 ℃, experts also need to assess damage from the vast quantities of water firefighters poured into the cathedral. One casualty of this was The Great Organ constructed in the 1730s, which was said to have escaped the flames but been significantly damaged by water."}, {"label": "K", "text": "French political commentators noted the devastating fire had succeeded where Macron had failed in uniting the country. But criticism over the original state of the building is likely to intensify over coming days. Leniaud told La Croix newspaper: \"This is not about looking for people to blame. The responsibility is collective because this is the most loved monument in the country.\" Alexandre Gady, an art historian, agreed. \"We've been saying for years that the budget for maintaining historic monuments is too low,\" Gady said. The Paris prosecutor's office has opened an inquiry into \"involuntary destruction by fire\", indicating they believe the cause of the blaze was accidental rather than criminal."}], "readingPassages": {"46": "We often think of drawing as something that takes inborn talent, but this kind of thinking stems from our misclassification of drawing as, primarily, an art form rather than a tool for learning.\nResearchers, teachers, and artists are starting to see how drawing can positively impact a wide variety of skills and disciplines.\nMost of us have spent some time drawing before, but at some point, most of us stop drawing. There are people who don't, obviously, and thank God for that: a world without designers and artists would be a very shabby one indeed.\nSome argue that so many adults have abandoned drawing because we've miscategorized it and given it a very narrow definition. In his book, Stick Figures: Drawing as a Human Practice, Professor D. B. Dowd argues that we have misfiled the significance of drawing because we see it as a professional skill instead of a personal capacity. We mistakenly think of \"good\" drawings as those which work as recreations of the real world, as realistic illusions. Rather, drawing should be recategorized as a symbolic tool.\nHuman beings have been drawing for 73,000 years. It's part of what it means to be human. We don't have the strength of chimpanzees(大猩猩) because we've given up animal strength to manipulate subtle instruments, like hammers, spears, and—later—pens and pencils. The human hand is an extremely dense network of nerve endings. In many ways, human beings are built to draw.\nSome researchers argue that doodling(涂画) activates the brain's so-called default circuit—essentially, the areas of the brain responsible for maintaining a baseline level of activity in the absence of other stimuli. Because of this, some believe that doodling during a boring lecture can help students pay attention. In one study, participants were asked to listen to a list of names while either doodling or sitting still. Those who doodled remembered 29 percent more of the names than those who did not.\nThere's also evidence that drawing talent is based on how accurately someone perceives the world. The human visual system tends to misjudge size, shape, color, and angles but artists perceive these qualities more accurately than non-artists. Cultivating drawing talent can become an essential tool to improve people's observational skills in fields where the visual is important.\nRather than think of drawing as a talent that some creative people are gifted in, we should consider it as a tool for seeing and understanding the world better—one that just so happens to double as an art form. Both absent-minded doodling and copying from life have been shown to positively affect your memory and visual perception, so complain loudly the next time your school board slashes the art department's budget.", "47": "We often think of drawing as something that takes inborn talent, but this kind of thinking stems from our misclassification of drawing as, primarily, an art form rather than a tool for learning.\nResearchers, teachers, and artists are starting to see how drawing can positively impact a wide variety of skills and disciplines.\nMost of us have spent some time drawing before, but at some point, most of us stop drawing. There are people who don't, obviously, and thank God for that: a world without designers and artists would be a very shabby one indeed.\nSome argue that so many adults have abandoned drawing because we've miscategorized it and given it a very narrow definition. In his book, Stick Figures: Drawing as a Human Practice, Professor D. B. Dowd argues that we have misfiled the significance of drawing because we see it as a professional skill instead of a personal capacity. We mistakenly think of \"good\" drawings as those which work as recreations of the real world, as realistic illusions. Rather, drawing should be recategorized as a symbolic tool.\nHuman beings have been drawing for 73,000 years. It's part of what it means to be human. We don't have the strength of chimpanzees(大猩猩) because we've given up animal strength to manipulate subtle instruments, like hammers, spears, and—later—pens and pencils. The human hand is an extremely dense network of nerve endings. In many ways, human beings are built to draw.\nSome researchers argue that doodling(涂画) activates the brain's so-called default circuit—essentially, the areas of the brain responsible for maintaining a baseline level of activity in the absence of other stimuli. Because of this, some believe that doodling during a boring lecture can help students pay attention. In one study, participants were asked to listen to a list of names while either doodling or sitting still. Those who doodled remembered 29 percent more of the names than those who did not.\nThere's also evidence that drawing talent is based on how accurately someone perceives the world. The human visual system tends to misjudge size, shape, color, and angles but artists perceive these qualities more accurately than non-artists. Cultivating drawing talent can become an essential tool to improve people's observational skills in fields where the visual is important.\nRather than think of drawing as a talent that some creative people are gifted in, we should consider it as a tool for seeing and understanding the world better—one that just so happens to double as an art form. Both absent-minded doodling and copying from life have been shown to positively affect your memory and visual perception, so complain loudly the next time your school board slashes the art department's budget.", "48": "We often think of drawing as something that takes inborn talent, but this kind of thinking stems from our misclassification of drawing as, primarily, an art form rather than a tool for learning.\nResearchers, teachers, and artists are starting to see how drawing can positively impact a wide variety of skills and disciplines.\nMost of us have spent some time drawing before, but at some point, most of us stop drawing. There are people who don't, obviously, and thank God for that: a world without designers and artists would be a very shabby one indeed.\nSome argue that so many adults have abandoned drawing because we've miscategorized it and given it a very narrow definition. In his book, Stick Figures: Drawing as a Human Practice, Professor D. B. Dowd argues that we have misfiled the significance of drawing because we see it as a professional skill instead of a personal capacity. We mistakenly think of \"good\" drawings as those which work as recreations of the real world, as realistic illusions. Rather, drawing should be recategorized as a symbolic tool.\nHuman beings have been drawing for 73,000 years. It's part of what it means to be human. We don't have the strength of chimpanzees(大猩猩) because we've given up animal strength to manipulate subtle instruments, like hammers, spears, and—later—pens and pencils. The human hand is an extremely dense network of nerve endings. In many ways, human beings are built to draw.\nSome researchers argue that doodling(涂画) activates the brain's so-called default circuit—essentially, the areas of the brain responsible for maintaining a baseline level of activity in the absence of other stimuli. Because of this, some believe that doodling during a boring lecture can help students pay attention. In one study, participants were asked to listen to a list of names while either doodling or sitting still. Those who doodled remembered 29 percent more of the names than those who did not.\nThere's also evidence that drawing talent is based on how accurately someone perceives the world. The human visual system tends to misjudge size, shape, color, and angles but artists perceive these qualities more accurately than non-artists. Cultivating drawing talent can become an essential tool to improve people's observational skills in fields where the visual is important.\nRather than think of drawing as a talent that some creative people are gifted in, we should consider it as a tool for seeing and understanding the world better—one that just so happens to double as an art form. Both absent-minded doodling and copying from life have been shown to positively affect your memory and visual perception, so complain loudly the next time your school board slashes the art department's budget.", "49": "We often think of drawing as something that takes inborn talent, but this kind of thinking stems from our misclassification of drawing as, primarily, an art form rather than a tool for learning.\nResearchers, teachers, and artists are starting to see how drawing can positively impact a wide variety of skills and disciplines.\nMost of us have spent some time drawing before, but at some point, most of us stop drawing. There are people who don't, obviously, and thank God for that: a world without designers and artists would be a very shabby one indeed.\nSome argue that so many adults have abandoned drawing because we've miscategorized it and given it a very narrow definition. In his book, Stick Figures: Drawing as a Human Practice, Professor D. B. Dowd argues that we have misfiled the significance of drawing because we see it as a professional skill instead of a personal capacity. We mistakenly think of \"good\" drawings as those which work as recreations of the real world, as realistic illusions. Rather, drawing should be recategorized as a symbolic tool.\nHuman beings have been drawing for 73,000 years. It's part of what it means to be human. We don't have the strength of chimpanzees(大猩猩) because we've given up animal strength to manipulate subtle instruments, like hammers, spears, and—later—pens and pencils. The human hand is an extremely dense network of nerve endings. In many ways, human beings are built to draw.\nSome researchers argue that doodling(涂画) activates the brain's so-called default circuit—essentially, the areas of the brain responsible for maintaining a baseline level of activity in the absence of other stimuli. Because of this, some believe that doodling during a boring lecture can help students pay attention. In one study, participants were asked to listen to a list of names while either doodling or sitting still. Those who doodled remembered 29 percent more of the names than those who did not.\nThere's also evidence that drawing talent is based on how accurately someone perceives the world. The human visual system tends to misjudge size, shape, color, and angles but artists perceive these qualities more accurately than non-artists. Cultivating drawing talent can become an essential tool to improve people's observational skills in fields where the visual is important.\nRather than think of drawing as a talent that some creative people are gifted in, we should consider it as a tool for seeing and understanding the world better—one that just so happens to double as an art form. Both absent-minded doodling and copying from life have been shown to positively affect your memory and visual perception, so complain loudly the next time your school board slashes the art department's budget.", "50": "We often think of drawing as something that takes inborn talent, but this kind of thinking stems from our misclassification of drawing as, primarily, an art form rather than a tool for learning.\nResearchers, teachers, and artists are starting to see how drawing can positively impact a wide variety of skills and disciplines.\nMost of us have spent some time drawing before, but at some point, most of us stop drawing. There are people who don't, obviously, and thank God for that: a world without designers and artists would be a very shabby one indeed.\nSome argue that so many adults have abandoned drawing because we've miscategorized it and given it a very narrow definition. In his book, Stick Figures: Drawing as a Human Practice, Professor D. B. Dowd argues that we have misfiled the significance of drawing because we see it as a professional skill instead of a personal capacity. We mistakenly think of \"good\" drawings as those which work as recreations of the real world, as realistic illusions. Rather, drawing should be recategorized as a symbolic tool.\nHuman beings have been drawing for 73,000 years. It's part of what it means to be human. We don't have the strength of chimpanzees(大猩猩) because we've given up animal strength to manipulate subtle instruments, like hammers, spears, and—later—pens and pencils. The human hand is an extremely dense network of nerve endings. In many ways, human beings are built to draw.\nSome researchers argue that doodling(涂画) activates the brain's so-called default circuit—essentially, the areas of the brain responsible for maintaining a baseline level of activity in the absence of other stimuli. Because of this, some believe that doodling during a boring lecture can help students pay attention. In one study, participants were asked to listen to a list of names while either doodling or sitting still. Those who doodled remembered 29 percent more of the names than those who did not.\nThere's also evidence that drawing talent is based on how accurately someone perceives the world. The human visual system tends to misjudge size, shape, color, and angles but artists perceive these qualities more accurately than non-artists. Cultivating drawing talent can become an essential tool to improve people's observational skills in fields where the visual is important.\nRather than think of drawing as a talent that some creative people are gifted in, we should consider it as a tool for seeing and understanding the world better—one that just so happens to double as an art form. Both absent-minded doodling and copying from life have been shown to positively affect your memory and visual perception, so complain loudly the next time your school board slashes the art department's budget.", "51": "The car has reshaped our cities. It seems to offer autonomy for everyone. There is something almost delightful in the detachment from reality of advertisements showing mass-produced cars marketed as symbols of individuality and of freedom when most of their lives will be spent making short journeys on choked roads.\nFor all the fuss made about top speeds, cornering ability and acceleration, the most useful gadgets on a modern car are those which work when you're going very slowly: parking sensors, sound systems, and navigation apps which will show a way around upcoming traffic jams. This seems to be one of the few areas where the benefit of sharing personal information comes straight back to the sharer: because these apps know where almost all the users are, and how fast they are moving almost all the time, they can spot traffic congestion(堵塞) very quickly and suggest ways round it.\nThe problem comes when everyone is using a navigation app which tells them to avoid everyone else using the same gadget. Traffic jams often appear where no one has enough information to avoid them. When a lucky few have access to the knowledge, they will benefit greatly. But when everyone has perfect information, traffic jams simply spread onto the side roads that seem to offer a way round them.\nThis new congestion teaches us two things. The first is that the promises of technology will never be realised as fully as we hope; they will be limited by their unforeseen and unintended consequences. Sitting in a more comfortable car in a different traffic jam is pleasant but hardly the liberation that once seemed to be promised. The second is that self-organisation will not get us where we want to go. The efforts of millions of drivers to get ahead do not miraculously produce a situation in which everyone does better than before, but one in which almost everyone does rather worse. Central control and collective organisation can produce smoother and fairer outcomes, though even that much is never guaranteed.\nSimilar limits can be foreseen for the much greater advances promised by self-driving cars. Last week, one operated by the taxi company Uber struck and killed a woman pushing her bicycle across a wide road in Arizona. This was the first recorded death involving a car which was supposed to be fully autonomous. Experts have said that it suggests a \"catastrophic failure\" of technology.\nIncreasingly, even Silicon Valley has to acknowledge the costs of the intoxicating(令人陶醉的) hurry that characterises its culture. What traffic teaches us is that reckless and uncontrolled change is as likely to harm us as it is to benefit us, and that thoughtful regulation is necessary for a better future.", "52": "The car has reshaped our cities. It seems to offer autonomy for everyone. There is something almost delightful in the detachment from reality of advertisements showing mass-produced cars marketed as symbols of individuality and of freedom when most of their lives will be spent making short journeys on choked roads.\nFor all the fuss made about top speeds, cornering ability and acceleration, the most useful gadgets on a modern car are those which work when you're going very slowly: parking sensors, sound systems, and navigation apps which will show a way around upcoming traffic jams. This seems to be one of the few areas where the benefit of sharing personal information comes straight back to the sharer: because these apps know where almost all the users are, and how fast they are moving almost all the time, they can spot traffic congestion(堵塞) very quickly and suggest ways round it.\nThe problem comes when everyone is using a navigation app which tells them to avoid everyone else using the same gadget. Traffic jams often appear where no one has enough information to avoid them. When a lucky few have access to the knowledge, they will benefit greatly. But when everyone has perfect information, traffic jams simply spread onto the side roads that seem to offer a way round them.\nThis new congestion teaches us two things. The first is that the promises of technology will never be realised as fully as we hope; they will be limited by their unforeseen and unintended consequences. Sitting in a more comfortable car in a different traffic jam is pleasant but hardly the liberation that once seemed to be promised. The second is that self-organisation will not get us where we want to go. The efforts of millions of drivers to get ahead do not miraculously produce a situation in which everyone does better than before, but one in which almost everyone does rather worse. Central control and collective organisation can produce smoother and fairer outcomes, though even that much is never guaranteed.\nSimilar limits can be foreseen for the much greater advances promised by self-driving cars. Last week, one operated by the taxi company Uber struck and killed a woman pushing her bicycle across a wide road in Arizona. This was the first recorded death involving a car which was supposed to be fully autonomous. Experts have said that it suggests a \"catastrophic failure\" of technology.\nIncreasingly, even Silicon Valley has to acknowledge the costs of the intoxicating(令人陶醉的) hurry that characterises its culture. What traffic teaches us is that reckless and uncontrolled change is as likely to harm us as it is to benefit us, and that thoughtful regulation is necessary for a better future.", "53": "The car has reshaped our cities. It seems to offer autonomy for everyone. There is something almost delightful in the detachment from reality of advertisements showing mass-produced cars marketed as symbols of individuality and of freedom when most of their lives will be spent making short journeys on choked roads.\nFor all the fuss made about top speeds, cornering ability and acceleration, the most useful gadgets on a modern car are those which work when you're going very slowly: parking sensors, sound systems, and navigation apps which will show a way around upcoming traffic jams. This seems to be one of the few areas where the benefit of sharing personal information comes straight back to the sharer: because these apps know where almost all the users are, and how fast they are moving almost all the time, they can spot traffic congestion(堵塞) very quickly and suggest ways round it.\nThe problem comes when everyone is using a navigation app which tells them to avoid everyone else using the same gadget. Traffic jams often appear where no one has enough information to avoid them. When a lucky few have access to the knowledge, they will benefit greatly. But when everyone has perfect information, traffic jams simply spread onto the side roads that seem to offer a way round them.\nThis new congestion teaches us two things. The first is that the promises of technology will never be realised as fully as we hope; they will be limited by their unforeseen and unintended consequences. Sitting in a more comfortable car in a different traffic jam is pleasant but hardly the liberation that once seemed to be promised. The second is that self-organisation will not get us where we want to go. The efforts of millions of drivers to get ahead do not miraculously produce a situation in which everyone does better than before, but one in which almost everyone does rather worse. Central control and collective organisation can produce smoother and fairer outcomes, though even that much is never guaranteed.\nSimilar limits can be foreseen for the much greater advances promised by self-driving cars. Last week, one operated by the taxi company Uber struck and killed a woman pushing her bicycle across a wide road in Arizona. This was the first recorded death involving a car which was supposed to be fully autonomous. Experts have said that it suggests a \"catastrophic failure\" of technology.\nIncreasingly, even Silicon Valley has to acknowledge the costs of the intoxicating(令人陶醉的) hurry that characterises its culture. What traffic teaches us is that reckless and uncontrolled change is as likely to harm us as it is to benefit us, and that thoughtful regulation is necessary for a better future.", "54": "The car has reshaped our cities. It seems to offer autonomy for everyone. There is something almost delightful in the detachment from reality of advertisements showing mass-produced cars marketed as symbols of individuality and of freedom when most of their lives will be spent making short journeys on choked roads.\nFor all the fuss made about top speeds, cornering ability and acceleration, the most useful gadgets on a modern car are those which work when you're going very slowly: parking sensors, sound systems, and navigation apps which will show a way around upcoming traffic jams. This seems to be one of the few areas where the benefit of sharing personal information comes straight back to the sharer: because these apps know where almost all the users are, and how fast they are moving almost all the time, they can spot traffic congestion(堵塞) very quickly and suggest ways round it.\nThe problem comes when everyone is using a navigation app which tells them to avoid everyone else using the same gadget. Traffic jams often appear where no one has enough information to avoid them. When a lucky few have access to the knowledge, they will benefit greatly. But when everyone has perfect information, traffic jams simply spread onto the side roads that seem to offer a way round them.\nThis new congestion teaches us two things. The first is that the promises of technology will never be realised as fully as we hope; they will be limited by their unforeseen and unintended consequences. Sitting in a more comfortable car in a different traffic jam is pleasant but hardly the liberation that once seemed to be promised. The second is that self-organisation will not get us where we want to go. The efforts of millions of drivers to get ahead do not miraculously produce a situation in which everyone does better than before, but one in which almost everyone does rather worse. Central control and collective organisation can produce smoother and fairer outcomes, though even that much is never guaranteed.\nSimilar limits can be foreseen for the much greater advances promised by self-driving cars. Last week, one operated by the taxi company Uber struck and killed a woman pushing her bicycle across a wide road in Arizona. This was the first recorded death involving a car which was supposed to be fully autonomous. Experts have said that it suggests a \"catastrophic failure\" of technology.\nIncreasingly, even Silicon Valley has to acknowledge the costs of the intoxicating(令人陶醉的) hurry that characterises its culture. What traffic teaches us is that reckless and uncontrolled change is as likely to harm us as it is to benefit us, and that thoughtful regulation is necessary for a better future.", "55": "The car has reshaped our cities. It seems to offer autonomy for everyone. There is something almost delightful in the detachment from reality of advertisements showing mass-produced cars marketed as symbols of individuality and of freedom when most of their lives will be spent making short journeys on choked roads.\nFor all the fuss made about top speeds, cornering ability and acceleration, the most useful gadgets on a modern car are those which work when you're going very slowly: parking sensors, sound systems, and navigation apps which will show a way around upcoming traffic jams. This seems to be one of the few areas where the benefit of sharing personal information comes straight back to the sharer: because these apps know where almost all the users are, and how fast they are moving almost all the time, they can spot traffic congestion(堵塞) very quickly and suggest ways round it.\nThe problem comes when everyone is using a navigation app which tells them to avoid everyone else using the same gadget. Traffic jams often appear where no one has enough information to avoid them. When a lucky few have access to the knowledge, they will benefit greatly. But when everyone has perfect information, traffic jams simply spread onto the side roads that seem to offer a way round them.\nThis new congestion teaches us two things. The first is that the promises of technology will never be realised as fully as we hope; they will be limited by their unforeseen and unintended consequences. Sitting in a more comfortable car in a different traffic jam is pleasant but hardly the liberation that once seemed to be promised. The second is that self-organisation will not get us where we want to go. The efforts of millions of drivers to get ahead do not miraculously produce a situation in which everyone does better than before, but one in which almost everyone does rather worse. Central control and collective organisation can produce smoother and fairer outcomes, though even that much is never guaranteed.\nSimilar limits can be foreseen for the much greater advances promised by self-driving cars. Last week, one operated by the taxi company Uber struck and killed a woman pushing her bicycle across a wide road in Arizona. This was the first recorded death involving a car which was supposed to be fully autonomous. Experts have said that it suggests a \"catastrophic failure\" of technology.\nIncreasingly, even Silicon Valley has to acknowledge the costs of the intoxicating(令人陶醉的) hurry that characterises its culture. What traffic teaches us is that reckless and uncontrolled change is as likely to harm us as it is to benefit us, and that thoughtful regulation is necessary for a better future."}, "questions": [{"id": "2021-06-2-l-1", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 1, "stem": "How does the woman describe her previous company's culture?", "options": ["A) Weird.", "B) Efficient.", "C) Tolerant.", "D) Toxic."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （态度观点题） Toxic.\n【定位】The problem with a company culture that prizes directness above all else is that it creates a\ntoxic culture of brilliant jerks that drives people out and erodes itself from within.\n一种把“直接”奉为高于一切的公司文化，问题在于它会造就一种由“才华横溢的混蛋”构成的\n有毒文化，逼走员工，并从内部把自己腐蚀掉。\n【信号】被问者亲口的定性词。女士在谈她前公司时，自己直接说出 it creates a toxic culture，\n“toxic”就是她对那种文化的定性，是被问一方的直接评价，正是答案句的信号。\n【替换】it creates a toxic culture ↔ Toxic.\n原句形容词直接成选项\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n录音确实念了 in the name of efficiency，efficient 听着耳熟极易勾选；但那是说前公司\n“打着效率的旗号”给苛刻反馈，是手段不是女士对文化的评价，属典型原词陷阱。\n借用原文：in the name of efficiency\nC) 张冠李戴\nTolerant（宽容）更接近男士公司“accommodating and kind”那种“迁就”的描述，张冠李\n戴到女士的前公司头上了；女士前公司的特点是“直接”，不是宽容。\n借用原文：accommodating and kind\nA) 未提及\nWeird（古怪）在录音里没有任何对应，女士对前公司的评价是“toxic”而非“古怪”，属无中\n生有。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-l-2", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 2, "stem": "What does the man say about his company's managers?", "options": ["A) They are arrogant.", "B) They are ignorant.", "C) They are ambitious.", "D) They are accommodating."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） They are accommodating.\n【定位】My company's managers tend to be accommodating and kind, overlooking mistakes or issues\nso as not to hurt feelings.\n我们公司的管理者往往随和又和善，会忽略错误或问题，免得伤了大家的感情。\n【信号】男士直接陈述自家管理者。男士主语直接是 My company's managers tend to be\naccommodating，紧扣题问的“他公司的管理者”，是说话人对该对象的直接陈述句，定位\n明确。\n【替换】accommodating and kind ↔ They are accommodating.\n原文形容词直接入选项\n【排除】B) 偷换概念\nignorant（无知）与录音里多次出现的 \"overlooking/ignored”（忽略）读音、含义都沾\n边，听到 overlooking mistakes 极易误推成“无知”；但男士说的是管理者“故意忽略问题”，\n不是“无知”，是音/义双重误导。\n借用原文：overlooking mistakes or issues\nA) 与原文相反\nArrogant（傲慢）与男士说的“accommodating and kind”（随和和善）完全相反，男士对\n自家管理者并无负面评价，选它等于把意思听反了。\n借用原文：accommodating and kind\nC) 未提及\nAmbitious（有野心/有抱负）在男士谈管理者的话里没有任何对应，男士说的是“随和、怕\n伤感情”，与“野心”无关，属无中生有。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-l-3", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 3, "stem": "What does the woman say the employees in her new company can do?", "options": ["A) They can think big.", "B) They can air their views.", "C) They can break conventions.", "D) They can work flexible hours."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） They can air their views.\n【定位】Employees have the power to speak up, give feedback, disagree and discuss problems in\nreal time.\n员工有权当场发声、给出反馈、表达不同意见并讨论问题。\n【信号】授权表达 have the power to。Employees have the power to speak up 用“have the\npower to”明确点出员工“有权做某事”，正是“can do”题要找的授权信号，后面罗列的动作即\n答案来源。\n【替换】speak up, give feedback, disagree ↔ They can air their views.\n概括同义改写\n【排除】D) 未提及\nflexible hours（弹性工时）听着像职场福利、很诱人，但录音通篇讲的是“反馈/发声”，从\n没提工作时间，属凭印象脑补的无中生有。\nC) 过度推断\nbreak conventions（打破常规）比原文的“disagree/speak up”拔得过高，员工只是“能表\n异议、讨论问题”，并没有说他们“打破常规”，是过度推断。\n借用原文：disagree and discuss problems\nA) 未提及\nthink big（有大格局/想大事）原文没有对应，员工被赋予的是“发声、给反馈”的权利，与\n“想得大”无关，属无中生有兼拔高。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-l-4", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 4, "stem": "What does the man say about constructive feedback?", "options": ["A) It can alter people's mindsets.", "B) It can lead to new discoveries.", "C) It enables people to learn and grow.", "D) It is conducive to critical thinking."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） It enables people to learn and grow.\n【定位】Constructive feedback, after all, is how we learn and grow.\n毕竟，建设性反馈正是我们学习和成长的途径。\n【信号】结论口吻 after all + is how we。Constructive feedback, after all, is how we learn and\ngrow 用 after all 引出总结、is how we 给出定性结论，是男士对反馈作用下的核心论断，\n正是“It can…”题要找的结论句。\n【替换】is how we learn and grow ↔ It enables people to learn and grow.\n句式转换同义\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\n录音里 mindset 确实被念到——但那是女士说的 people need to shift their mindsets\n（人要转变接受反馈的心态），不是男士说“反馈能改变心态”；既不是男士说的，作用对象\n也不同，属张冠李戴式原词陷阱。\n借用原文：people need to shift their mindsets\nD) 未提及\ncritical thinking（批判性思维）原文从未出现，男士说的是“学习与成长”，硬扯到“批判性\n思维”是无中生有兼概念拔高。\nB) 未提及\nnew discoveries（新发现）录音里没有任何对应，男士谈的是反馈对“育儿、友谊、职业发\n展”的益处，与“新发现”无关，属未提及。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-l-5", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 5, "stem": "What does the man say about his holiday?", "options": ["A) He kept looking for the best place to stay.", "B) He met many tourists from other countries.", "C) He had a great time sightseeing and relaxing.", "D) He managed to visit a different city each day."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） He kept looking for the best place to stay.\n【定位】But I found myself moving from one accommodation to another, trying to find the perfect\nplace. Instead of relaxing, I was looking for the best spot.\n但我发现自己从一个住处搬到另一个住处，想找到那个完美的地方。我没有放松，而是一直\n在找最好的落脚点。\n【信号】转折 But + 对比 Instead of。答案句的信号是双重的：先有转折 But I found myself\nmoving from one accommodation to another，把\"假期\"的真实状态从\"轻松\"拽向\"折\n腾\"；再有对比 Instead of relaxing, I was looking for the best spot，明说\"不是放松、而\n是在找最好的\"——转折与\"Instead of A, B\"句式叠加处，正是他对假期的真正定调。\n【替换】trying to find the perfect place ↔ looking for the best place to stay\nperfect=best，place 同指住处\nmoving from one accommodation to another ↔ He kept looking\n反复搬=持续地找(kept)\n【排除】C) 与原文相反\nC 的 He had a great time sightseeing and relaxing. 偷了录音里的原词 relaxing 来勾你\n选，可原文是 Instead of relaxing, I was looking for the best spot——relaxing 前面正\n挂着 Instead of（不是…而是），意思恰恰是\"没在放松\"。听到 relaxing 就选，正好掉\n进\"原词被否定\"的坑。\n借用原文：Instead of relaxing, I was looking for the best spot\nB) 原词陷阱\nB 的 He met many tourists from other countries. 听上去呼应开头那句 Not too many\nother tourists around, were there? 里的 tourists，但原文男士回答的是 No, very few\nrelatively（游客很少），并未说\"遇到很多别国游客\"；这是借 tourists 一词做的答非所问。\n借用原文：No, very few relatively\nD) 未提及\nD 的 He managed to visit a different city each day. 描绘\"每天去不同城市\"，但男士全程\n只谈\"换住处找最好的\"（accommodation / best spot），从没提过\"每天换城市\"。它\n用\"不停移动\"的画面感来诱你联想，实属未提及。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-l-6", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 6, "stem": "What does the woman say people tend to do when making decisions?", "options": ["A) Prioritize what is essential to their best advantage.", "B) Stretch out the process in search of the optimal.", "C) Deliberate the consequences that may occur.", "D) Take all relevant factors into consideration."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Stretch out the process in search of the optimal.\n【定位】Essentially, we have this tendency to keep stretching out the decision-making process,\nbecause as human beings, we are hard-wired to optimize.\n本质上，我们有一种不断拖长决策过程的倾向，因为作为人类，我们天生就被设定为追求\n最优。\n【信号】tendency to + 总结词 Essentially。答案信号是\"总结+点天性\"：Essentially（本质上）\n领出结论，紧跟 we have this tendency to keep stretching out the decision-making\nprocess 直接点出\"人有拖长决策过程的倾向\"，tendency 正对题干 tend to；后半\nbecause…hard-wired to optimize 补出\"为什么\"，整句就是\"人们做决定时倾向做什么\"的\n标准答案句。\n【替换】keep stretching out the decision-making process ↔ Stretch out the process\n近乎同形，只略作删减\nhard-wired to optimize ↔ in search of the optimal\noptimize 与 optimal 同根，动词转名词\n【排除】A) 未提及\nA 的 Prioritize what is essential to their best advantage. 用 best advantage 蹭整段反\n复出现的 best/optimize 之感，但原文说的是\"人会拖长过程\"，不是\"优先抓要紧事\"；\nprioritize（优先排序）这层意思原文从未提及，是借相近语感造的干扰。\nC) 偷换概念\nC 的 Deliberate the consequences that may occur.（仔细考虑可能的后果）听着也像\"决\n策时的做法\"，但原文 hard-wired to optimize 讲的是\"追求最优\"，根本没提\"权衡后果\"，\nconsequences 是凭空添的概念。\n借用原文：hard-wired to optimize\nD) 未提及\nD 的 Take all relevant factors into consideration. 是个放哪都对的万能说法，但原文女士\n说的是\"人倾向拖长过程\"，并未说\"人会把所有相关因素都纳入考虑\"；它靠\"听起来很对\"诱\n选，实为未提及。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-l-7", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 7, "stem": "What has made decision making increasingly difficult?", "options": ["A) Time pressure.", "B) Tight budget.", "C) Modern technology.", "D) Fierce competition."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Modern technology.\n【定位】But I think thanks to technology, we can make comparisons more easily and have more\naccess to choice and customization. We keep looking over and return to the same options\nagain and again.\n但我觉得，多亏了科技，我们能更容易地做比较，能接触到更多选择和定制。我们一遍又一\n遍地翻看、回到同样的选项上。\n【信号】因果信号 thanks to。答案信号是因果短语 thanks to technology——thanks to（多亏、\n由于）直接引出原因 technology，其后 we can make comparisons more easily and\nhave more access to choice and customization 说明科技带来\"选择更多、更难定\"的后\n果，正是\"什么让决策变难\"的答案句。\n【替换】thanks to technology ↔ Modern technology\ntechnology 加修饰 Modern，同指一物\nhave more access to choice and customization ↔ Modern technology\n\"选择变多\"是科技致难的内在理由\n【排除】A) 未提及\nA 的 Time pressure. 是\"做事难\"时最顺常识的猜想，但录音从头到尾没把\"时间压力\"列为决\n策变难的原因，反倒第 7 段女士主张\"要投入时间去研究\"；Time pressure 纯属凭常识脑\n补，未提及。\nB) 未提及\nB 的 Tight budget. 把成因引向\"钱不够\"，但全程谈的是\"选择太多、反复纠结\"，从没提过\n预算或花钱，属凭空添加的原因，未提及。\nD) 未提及\nD 的 Fierce competition. 同属\"现代社会通病\"类词、听感和 Modern technology 一样\"像\n那么回事\"，但录音从未把 competition 当成决策变难的原因，是借相近气质设的干扰，未\n提及。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-l-8", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 8, "stem": "According to the woman, what should people do when making important decisions?", "options": ["A) Research as many different options as possible.", "B) Avoid going over the same options repeatedly.", "C) Focus on what is practical.", "D) Trust their gut instinct."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Avoid going over the same options repeatedly.\n【定位】What you need to do is research: have a process, invest time exploring your options, and\neliminate as many things as you can. The most toxic part of decision making is going over the\nsame options time and time again.\n你需要做的是去研究：有一套流程，投入时间去探索你的各种选项，并尽可能多地排除掉一\n些。决策中最有害的部分，就是一遍又一遍地翻来覆去看同样的选项。\n【信号】建议句 What you need to do + 最高级 most toxic。答案信号有两处：What you need\nto do is research 是直接的建议句（你需要做的是…），亮出女士的主张；而真正对上 B 的\n是最高级评价 The most toxic part of decision making is going over the same options\ntime and time again——most toxic（最有害）点明\"反复纠结同一批选项\"是最该避免\n的，反推出\"应避免反复纠结\"这一建议。\n【替换】The most toxic part of decision making is going over the same options time and time again ↔\nAvoid going over the same options repeatedly\n\"最有害\"=应避免，肯否定转换\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 的 Research as many different options as possible. 偷用了原文 What you need to\ndo is research 的 research 来诱选，但女士的 research 强调的是\"有流程、投入时间、并\neliminate 排除掉尽量多的选项\"，而非 as many…as possible（尽可能多地研究选项）；A\n把\"排除\"说反成\"尽量多留\"，且没体现题问的\"重要决定\"落点，属盗原词的答非所问。\n借用原文：eliminate as many things as you can\nC) 原词陷阱\nC 的 Focus on what is practical. 用了原文出现的 practical，但原文是 When you have\n30 odd options, trusting your gut is not practical——practical 是用来说\"凭直觉不实\n际\"的，并非建议\"专注于实际的事\"；选项截走 practical 一词另造主张，属原词被挪用。\n借用原文：trusting your gut is not practical\nD) 与原文相反\nD 的 Trust their gut instinct. 直接搬来原文的 gut，但女士明说 gut instinct might be\noverrated、trusting your gut is not practical——直觉被高估、不实际，是被否定的做\n法。听到 gut instinct 就选，正中\"先抬后否\"的陷阱。\n借用原文：gut instinct might be overrated", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-l-9", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 9, "stem": "What did the 2006 research find about homework?", "options": ["A) It is beneficial to poor as well as rich communities.", "B) It is conducive to children's future development.", "C) It is welcome to parents but not to children.", "D) It is not of much help to younger children."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） It is not of much help to younger children.\n【定位】A 2006 research paper suggested some correlation between the amount of homework done by\na student and future academic achievement for middle and high school students but not so\nmuch for younger kids.\n一篇 2006 年的研究论文指出，对初高中学生而言，做作业的量与未来学业成就之间存在一\n定关联，但对更小的孩子来说，这种关联就没那么明显了。\n【信号】转折 but 之后才是落点。本句靠转折信号 but not so much for younger kids 定位：but\n之前讲初高中生有关联是铺垫，but 之后对低龄孩子的\"没那么有用\"才是该研究真正要强调\n的发现，也正是答案所在。\n【替换】not so much for younger kids ↔ not of much help to younger children\nnot so much 同义改写为 not of much help\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\npoor as well as rich communities 里的 communities 是录音第 2 段 affluent\ncommunities 才出现的词，与 2006 研究无关；本题问的是 2006 研究，A 把别处的词搬来\n凑，且\"贫富通吃\"在原文也无依据。\n借用原文：California's affluent communities\nB) 原词陷阱\nconducive to children's future development 借用了答案句前半 future academic\nachievement 的 future 一词，但那半句讲的是初高中生才有关联、是被 but 推翻的铺垫，\nB 误把铺垫当成总结论且笼统说成对所有 children 有益，与原文按年龄分层的发现不符。\n借用原文：future academic achievement for middle and high school students\nC) 未提及\nwelcome to parents but not to children 谈的是家长欢迎、孩子不欢迎的态度，录音第 1\n段只提到 Many parents and teachers are ardent supporters，从没说孩子的好恶，更没\n说 2006 研究有此发现，属凭空添加。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-l-10", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 10, "stem": "What do experts think of homework requiring internet access?", "options": ["A) It may put some students in remote areas at a disadvantage.", "B) It gives the majority of students ready access to their teachers.", "C) It effectively improves the learning quality of students in rural areas.", "D) It can bridge the learning gap between kids of different backgrounds."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （态度观点题） It may put some students in remote areas at a disadvantage.\n【定位】Studies found that in remote areas, the poor quality or lack of internet access can put students\nat a disadvantage because 70% of teachers in these areas assign homework that requires\ninternet access. ... Experts assert homework requiring the internet isn't fair.\n研究发现，在偏远地区，网络质量差或缺乏网络会使学生处于不利地位，因为这些地区\n70% 的教师布置的作业需要联网。……专家断言，需要联网的作业是不公平的。\n【信号】专家断言句 + isn't fair 评价词。定位靠两处信号：一是 Experts assert 直接对应题干问\n的\"专家看法\"，二是评价词 put students at a disadvantage 与 isn't fair 给出明确负面定\n性，正是态度题要抓的落点。\n【替换】can put students at a disadvantage ↔ put some students in remote areas at a disadvantage\n几乎同形的劣势表述对应\nisn't fair ↔ at a disadvantage\n不公平=处于劣势，负面同向\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nready access to their teachers 借了录音反复出现的 access 一词（internet access），却\n换成\"多数学生能便捷接触老师\"，原文谈的是网络接入而非接触老师，且强调的是大量人没\n有网络，B 既偷换概念又把困难说成便利。\n借用原文：lack of internet access\nC) 与原文相反\nimproves the learning quality of students in rural areas 借用 remote areas 同义的\nrural areas 谈农村学生，却说\"提升学习质量\"，与专家 isn't fair、at a disadvantage 的负\n面定性完全相反，是典型的正话反说。\n借用原文：can put students at a disadvantage\nD) 与原文相反\nbridge the learning gap between kids of different backgrounds 听起来很正面，但原文\n讲的是联网作业反而让缺网的偏远学生更吃亏、加大而非弥合差距，方向相反，且\"弥合差\n距\"原文从未表达。\n借用原文：put students at a disadvantage", "points": ["听力·篇章", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-l-11", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 11, "stem": "What conclusion could be drawn from the study in Germany?", "options": ["A) Diligent students tend to do their homework independently.", "B) The focus of homework should always be on school subjects.", "C) Doing homework exerts a positive effect on kids' personality development.", "D) The benefits of doing homework vary widely from individual to individual."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Doing homework exerts a positive effect on kids' personality development.\n【定位】A study in Germany found that homework could have an effect on students' personalities,\nsuggesting that doing homework might help kids to become more conscientious and\nindependent learners.\n德国的一项研究发现，作业可能对学生的性格产生影响，这表明做作业或许能帮助孩子成为\n更尽责、更独立的学习者。\n【信号】研究标志词 in Germany + suggesting 引出结论。定位靠 A study in Germany found\nthat 对应题干所问研究，再靠 suggesting that 引出该研究的结论；结论核心是 an effect\non students' personalities，即作业影响性格。\n【替换】an effect on students' personalities ↔ exerts a positive effect on kids' personality development\nhave an effect=exerts an effect，personalities=personality\nmore conscientious and independent learners ↔ a positive effect\n变得更尽责独立=正面影响\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nDiligent students tend to do their homework independently. 借了录音的\nindependent 一词，但原文说的是\"做作业使孩子变独立\"，A 反过来说\"勤奋学生倾向独立\n做作业\"，把因果方向掉了头，原文并无此说。\n借用原文：independent learners\nB) 未提及\nThe focus of homework should always be on school subjects. 含绝对词 always，谈的\n是作业内容该聚焦学科，而德国研究讲的是作业对性格的影响，根本没讨论作业内容该放在\n哪，属未提及加绝对化。\nD) 张冠李戴\nThe benefits of doing homework vary widely from individual to individual. 听起来稳\n妥，但德国研究的结论是统一指向\"影响性格\"，并未说作业益处因人而异；\"因个体差异\"更\n接近第 1、2 段按年龄/社区分层的内容，被错安到德国研究头上。\n借用原文：not so much for younger kids", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-l-12", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 12, "stem": "What do we learn about Goddard's idea of using rockets for space exploration?", "options": ["A) It was something he apologized for later.", "B) It was ridiculed by the New York Times.", "C) It was a forty-nine-year plan.", "D) It was considered visionary."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） It was ridiculed by the New York Times.\n【定位】In January 1920, The New York Times ridiculed Goddard's theory that rockets could be\nutilized for space exploration.\n1920年1月，《纽约时报》嘲笑了戈达德关于火箭可用于太空探索的理论。\n【信号】专有名词+负面动词定位。答案句的信号是专有名词 The New York Times 加一个鲜明的负\n面动词 ridiculed（嘲笑）。听到《纽约时报》这个被原样念出的机构名，再抓住紧跟的那\n个评价动词 ridiculed，这一句就是讲世人怎么看待他设想的答案句。\n【替换】The New York Times ridiculed Goddard's theory ↔ It was ridiculed by the New York Times\n主动改被动、对象不变\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\n录音里道歉的是《纽约时报》后来 published an apology to Goddard（向戈达德道\n歉），而 It was something he apologized for later 说成是戈达德自己为这设想道歉，主\n体被调换，正话说反。\n借用原文：the famed newspaper published an apology to Goddard\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音说 Forty-nine years later, Apollo 11 reached the Moon——49年指的是从被嘲笑到\n登月之间隔的年数，而 It was a forty-nine-year plan 硬把 49 说成一个49年的计划，盗用\n数字 49 却扭曲了它的含义。\n借用原文：Forty-nine years later, Apollo 11 reached the Moon\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音说 Like many visionaries（像许多有远见者一样），visionary 是形容戈达德这类人，\n且紧接着讲他遭遇质疑者；It was considered visionary 把 visionary 安到这个设想头上、\n说它当年被视为有远见，与录音随后描述的被嘲笑境遇相反。\n借用原文：Like many visionaries, the young scientist encountered numerous skeptics", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-l-13", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 13, "stem": "What does the passage say about Goddard's first rocket voyage?", "options": ["A) It was of great significance to rocket science.", "B) It was completed in the state of New Mexico.", "C) It was somehow delayed about 12 minutes.", "D) It failed due to a sudden change of weather."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） It was of great significance to rocket science.\n【定位】His maiden rocket voyage lasted a mere 3 seconds. It scaled an altitude of only 12 meters.\nNonetheless, it was a milestone in rocket science.\n他的首次火箭飞行仅持续了3秒，只达到12米的高度。尽管如此，它仍是火箭科学的一座里\n程碑。\n【信号】转折 Nonetheless 后才是定性。前两句净说短处（a mere 3 seconds、only 12\nmeters），真正给这次首飞定性的，是转折词 Nonetheless 之后那句——milestone（里\n程碑）。听力里 but / nonetheless 之后才是落点，前半多是铺垫与干扰。\n【替换】it was a milestone in rocket science ↔ It was of great significance to rocket science\n里程碑=意义重大的同义替换\n【排除】B) 张冠李戴\n录音明说首飞在 his native Massachusetts（家乡马萨诸塞），而 completed in the state\nof New Mexico 把地点换成新墨西哥——新墨西哥是他后来搬去的地方（第4段），与首飞\n不是一回事，地点张冠李戴。\n借用原文：Goddard launched his first rocket from an aunt's farm in his native Massachusetts\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音里 12 来自 an altitude of only 12 meters（高度仅12米），delayed about 12\nminutes 盗用数字 12，却把它从米偷换成延迟的分钟，单位和含义全变了。\n借用原文：It scaled an altitude of only 12 meters\nD) 与原文相反\n录音对首飞的定性是 it was a milestone（里程碑，成功），并未说失败，全段也没提天\n气；failed due to a sudden change of weather 既反了结果、又凭空加了天气原因。\n借用原文：Nonetheless, it was a milestone in rocket science", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-l-14", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 14, "stem": "Why did Goddard move to New Mexico?", "options": ["A) A laboratory and test range was already set up there.", "B) Its climate was ideal for year-round rocket launching.", "C) A weather expert invited him to go there for his mission.", "D) Its remote valleys were appealing to him and his family."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Its climate was ideal for year-round rocket launching.\n【定位】Goddard later consulted with a weather expert and determined that the climate of New\nMexico was ideal for year-round rocket launches.\n戈达德后来咨询了一位气象专家，断定新墨西哥的气候适合全年进行火箭发射。\n【信号】determined that 引出判断即原因。答案信号是 determined that the climate…was ideal\nfor year-round rocket launches——determined that 引出他做出的判断，这个判断（气\n候适合全年发射）正是促使他搬家的原因。听到这种表态度/判断的句子，原因就在其中。\n【替换】the climate of New Mexico was ideal for year-round rocket launches ↔\nIts climate was ideal for year-round rocket launching\n近乎原句的轻微改写\n【排除】A) 答非所问\n录音里 There he established a laboratory and test range（在那儿他建立了实验室和试\n验场）发生在搬过去之后，是结果不是原因；A laboratory and test range was already\nset up there 还加了 already（早已建好），与原文先搬后建的顺序相反。\n借用原文：There he established a laboratory and test range\nC) 过度推断\n录音是 consulted with a weather expert（他咨询了气象专家）以获取气候判断，而 A\nweather expert invited him to go there 把气象专家说成是邀请他去的人，凭空捏造了邀\n请这一动作。\n借用原文：Goddard later consulted with a weather expert\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音说他搬 to a remote valley in the southwest（搬到西南部一处偏僻山谷），remote\nvalley 只是描述落脚地，并未说山谷吸引了他全家；Its remote valleys were appealing to\nhim and his family 把地点描述硬说成搬家的吸引原因，且 appealing 系原文所无。\n借用原文：Goddard and his family relocated there, to a remote valley in the southwest of\nthe country", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-l-15", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 15, "stem": "What does the passage say about Goddard's achievements?", "options": ["A) He won an award from the US government for his work.", "B) He gained recognition from rocket scientists worldwide.", "C) He was granted over 200 patents in rocket technology.", "D) He boosted the military strength of the United States."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） He was granted over 200 patents in rocket technology.\n【定位】He was granted over 200 patents and continued to pioneer rocket technology until his death\nin 1945.\n他被授予了200多项专利，并持续开拓火箭技术，直到1945年去世。\n【信号】被原样念出的数字硬指标。答案句的信号是一个被原样念出的可量化硬指标 granted over\n200 patents（被授予200多项专利）。成就题里，这种数字+专有概念的句子最实、最难造\n假，听到 over 200 patents 立刻锁定它就是成就答案句。\n【替换】He was granted over 200 patents ↔ He was granted over 200 patents in rocket technology\n近原句、补限定语\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\n录音说 received negligible support from the government（几乎没得到政府支持），与\n得奖恰相反；He won an award from the US government 盗用 government 一词，却把\n没获政府支持反说成获政府颁奖。\n借用原文：the ambitious scientist received negligible support from the government\nB) 未提及\n录音只提资助他的富商和其他火箭爱好者（Other rocket enthusiasts also raised funds\nfor him），从未说他获得全球火箭科学家的认可；gained recognition from rocket\nscientists worldwide 是原文未提及的内容。\nD) 与原文相反\n录音说 was never able to interest the US military in rocket-propelled weapons（始终\n未能让美国军方对火箭动力武器感兴趣），与增强军力相反；He boosted the military\nstrength 盗用 military，却把军方不感兴趣反说成他增强了军力。\n借用原文：Goddard was never able to interest the US military in rocket-propelled weapons", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-l-16", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 16, "stem": "What does the speaker say about working for a new venture?", "options": ["A) It requires entrepreneurial experience.", "B) It is usually financially rewarding.", "C) It can be quite frustrating.", "D) It can be rather risky."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） It can be rather risky.\n【定位】Working for a new venture comes with a lot of risks. There's instability, unclear\nresponsibilities, and the need to be a master of all trades.\n为一家新公司工作伴随着大量风险。有不稳定、职责不清，还得样样都得自己上手。\n【信号】开篇定性句＋列举佐证。录音第一句就直接定性 comes with a lot of risks，随后用\ninstability, unclear responsibilities 列举来佐证「风险」，开篇主题句正是答案落点。\n【替换】comes with a lot of risks ↔ It can be rather risky.\n名词转形容词同义\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音确实多次提到创业者，结尾还有 the entrepreneur，但全文从没说「为新公司工作需\n要创业经验」；选项 It requires entrepreneurial experience. 盗用 entrepreneurial 这个\n高频原词，凑成一个录音没说的「前提条件」，听到熟词就选会中招。\n借用原文：the entrepreneur\nB) 偷换概念\n录音对「为新公司工作」先定性为有风险、不稳定，谈到好处时说的是 the passion and\nexcitement（激情与兴奋），而非金钱回报；It is usually financially rewarding. 把「精神\n回报」偷换成「经济回报」，方向相反。\n借用原文：the passion and excitement\nC) 未提及\n录音列举的负面是 instability, unclear responsibilities（客观处境），从没说工作「令人沮\n丧 frustrating」；It can be quite frustrating. 句式与正确项 D 几乎一样，靠「看起来也\n是负面」来骗选，但录音里并无 frustrating 之意。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-l-17", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 17, "stem": "What has science demonstrated regarding the positive culture of a new venture?", "options": ["A) It contributes to rapid business expansion.", "B) It inspires willingness to make sacrifices.", "C) It reduces conflict among team members.", "D) It encourages creation and innovation."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） It reduces conflict among team members.\n【定位】Science has already done a good job of proving the results that follow. These include better\nprocesses, greater team cohesion, reduced conflict and sharper alertness.\n科学已经很好地证明了随之而来的结果。这些结果包括更优的流程、更强的团队凝聚力、更\n少的冲突，以及更敏锐的警觉性。\n【信号】Science proving 后 These include 引出清单。信号词 Science has already done a\ngood job of proving 正对题干的 science demonstrated，随后 These include 引出一串\n结果，答案就藏在这个并列清单里。\n【替换】reduced conflict ↔ It reduces conflict among team members.\n过去分词转动词并补主语\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\n录音谈业务发展／增长是在后文讲第三阶段 developing the firm so it continues to grow\n时，且那是「激情与业绩」的研究，不属本段「科学已证明的文化结果」清单；It\ncontributes to rapid business expansion. 张冠李戴，把别处内容塞进本题。\n借用原文：developing the firm so it continues to grow\nB) 原词陷阱\n录音第二段说创始人的冒险精神 may inspire others to be more courageous，并无「愿\n意做出牺牲」被科学证明；It inspires willingness to make sacrifices. 借用了 inspire 这个\n原词和创业语境的「牺牲」印象，但科学证明清单里没有这一条。\n借用原文：The willingness to take risks may inspire others to be more courageous.\nD) 未提及\nIt encourages creation and innovation. 是创业话题最顺耳的套话，但录音 Science 证明\n的清单是 better processes, greater team cohesion, reduced conflict and sharper\nalertness，根本没有 creation／innovation；属凭印象脑补的未提及项。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-l-18", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 18, "stem": "What does the speaker say about entrepreneurs at the initial stage of a new venture?", "options": ["A) They have unrealistic expectations.", "B) They often work without any pay.", "C) Few can find willing investors.", "D) Many are idealistic dreamers."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） They often work without any pay.\n【定位】On many occasions, the entrepreneurs reported not paying themselves a wage at all initially,\nin order to cover salaries and expenses.\n在很多情况下，创业者表示自己起初根本不给自己开工资，为的是支付（员工）薪水和各项\n开支。\n【信号】时间词 initially 对上题干 initial stage。录音 not paying themselves a wage at all\ninitially 里的 initially 与题干的 initial stage 精确对应，加上 On many occasions（很多\n情况下）呼应选项的 often，是定位锚点。\n【替换】not paying themselves a wage at all initially ↔ They often work without any pay.\n不开工资即没有报酬地工作\nOn many occasions ↔ They often work without any pay.\n频率词同义\n【排除】A) 未提及\n录音谈创业者初期是「不给自己开工资以保员工薪水」，是务实的牺牲，全文没说他们「期\n望不切实际」；They have unrealistic expectations. 是凭「创业者爱做梦」的刻板印象脑\n补，录音无据。\nC) 偷换概念\n录音第七段确实讲到出钱的难处——paying them big dollars to attract them（高薪招\n人），但说的是创业者难以负担薪资，不是「找不到投资人」；Few can find willing\ninvestors. 借创业话题里的 investors 印象，把对象从「招员工／发工资」偷偷换成「找投\n资人」。\n借用原文：paying them big dollars to attract them\nD) 未提及\nMany are idealistic dreamers. 给创业者贴「理想主义梦想家」的标签，录音通篇讲的是创\n始人的 energy and enthusiasm（热情干劲）和务实牺牲，从没说他们是「空想家」；属\n凭印象脑补的未提及评价。\n借用原文：There's something about the founder's energy and enthusiasm that infects the rest of\nthe team.", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-l-19", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 19, "stem": "What do we learn about the possible reason why women tend to live longer?", "options": ["A) They have better dietary habits.", "B) They bear fewer social responsibilities.", "C) They are born with a stronger ability to socialize.", "D) They are better able to survive or handle disease."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） They are better able to survive or handle disease.\n【定位】No one is really certain of the reason for this, although the speculation centers around the idea\nthat women are more capable of surviving or handling disease than men.\n没人真正确定其中的原因，不过推测大多围绕这样一个想法：女性比男性更有能力在疾病中\n存活或应对疾病。\n【信号】原因解释信号 the reason…the speculation centers around the idea。这句用 the\nspeculation centers around the idea that（推测围绕这一想法）明确引出原因的内容，\n是典型的原因解释信号；女性更长寿的解释就落在这个 that 从句里。\n【替换】more capable of surviving or handling disease ↔ better able to survive or handle disease\nmore capable of = better able to，近义同构改写\n【排除】A) 未提及\nbetter dietary habits 是生活常识里「女性更长寿」的联想，但录音通篇没说饮食，属凭常\n识硬填的诱饵。\nB) 未提及\nThey bear fewer social responsibilities. 录音里没有任何关于承担社会责任多少的表述，\n是凭空捏造的方向。\nC) 原词陷阱\nborn with a stronger ability to socialize 借了下一段 nurture social connections 的社交\n字眼来诱选，但录音说的是女性倾向于经营社交、且作者还说这个解释难以信服，绝非天生\n社交力，既盗词又偷换。\n借用原文：women's immune systems benefit from their tendency to prioritize and nurture\nsocial connections", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-l-20", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 20, "stem": "What is the recent discovery about human cells?", "options": ["A) They have a limited reproductive ability.", "B) They depend on adequate sleep to thrive.", "C) They keep dividing throughout one's life.", "D) They strengthen with regular exercise."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） They have a limited reproductive ability.\n【定位】Only cancer cells, in fact, are capable of dividing forever. Human cells have a limited\nreproductive ability.\n事实上，只有癌细胞才能够永远分裂下去。人体细胞的繁殖能力是有限的。\n【信号】对比 only…+ 直陈结论句 Human cells have a limited…。Only cancer cells, in fact,\nare capable of dividing forever 用 only 把能永远分裂限定给癌细胞，反衬出紧接的直陈\n结论 Human cells have a limited reproductive ability——这句正面给出人体细胞的特\n性，是答案句。\n【替换】Human cells have a limited reproductive ability ↔ They have a limited reproductive ability.\n几乎原句对应，主语 Human cells 换成 They\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\ndepend on adequate sleep to thrive 借了录音 good sleep 这个词制造耳熟，但原文说\n睡眠只是推迟细胞停止分裂的手段之一，并非细胞靠睡眠才能茁壮的本质特性，听到 sleep\n就选会上当。\n借用原文：we can postpone the eventual stop of cells' dividing, through nutrition, exercise, good\nsleep, and even relaxation techniques\nC) 与原文相反\nThey keep dividing throughout one's life. 说人体细胞终生不停分裂，恰恰与录音相反\n——录音说 Only cancer cells, in fact, are capable of dividing forever，永远分裂是癌细\n胞专属，人体细胞反而有限，此项把对象和极性都弄反了。\n借用原文：Only cancer cells, in fact, are capable of dividing forever\nD) 原词陷阱\nThey strengthen with regular exercise. 借了录音 exercise 一词，但原文只把运动列为推\n迟细胞停止分裂的手段，从未说细胞会因运动而增强，是把原词嫁接到一个未说过的结论\n上。\n借用原文：we can postpone the eventual stop of cells' dividing, through nutrition, exercise,\ngood sleep", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-l-21", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 21, "stem": "What do many public health policy makers believe?", "options": ["A) The process of ageing can ultimately be brought under control.", "B) Improved health care for the elderly will contribute to longevity.", "C) Prevention of heart disease and stroke will increase life expectancy.", "D) The resolution of age-related diseases will solve the mystery of ageing."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） The resolution of age-related diseases will solve the mystery of ageing.\n【定位】Many public health policy makers believe that the resolution of age-associated disease will\ntell us something fundamental about the ageing process, but say some top scientists, that's\ncompletely erroneous.\n许多公共卫生政策制定者认为，解决与年龄相关的疾病会告诉我们关于衰老过程的某些根本\n性的东西，但一些顶尖科学家说，这完全是错误的。\n【信号】主体标志 Many public health policy makers believe that…。Many public health\npolicy makers believe that 直接点出题干所问的主体并带 believe，其后 that 从句就是他\n们的主张；这里要特别注意句中 but 之后是顶尖科学家的反驳，不属于被问方。\n【替换】the resolution of age-associated disease will tell us something fundamental about the ageing process\n↔ The resolution of age-related diseases will solve the mystery of ageing.\nage-associated=age-related，tell us something fundamental about=solve the mystery of\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nThe process of ageing can ultimately be brought under control. 说衰老终可被掌控，\n而录音明确 But we cannot stop the ageing process.、且全篇强调衰老仍是谜，此项方向\n与录音相反，更非政策制定者的主张。\n借用原文：But we cannot stop the ageing process.\nB) 未提及\nImproved health care for the elderly will contribute to longevity. 听来合理，但录音里\n政策制定者谈的是治好年龄相关疾病能否搞懂衰老，并未提改善老年照护带来长寿这一论\n断，属未提及。\nC) 原词陷阱\nPrevention of heart disease and stroke will increase life expectancy. 借了录音 heart\ndisease and stroke 这两个病名诱选，但原文提它们是顶尖科学家用来说明治这些病并不能\n告诉我们衰老的道理，且 would only add a decade to average life expectancy 说防住\n也仅多十年；此项既盗词，又把反驳语境错当政策制定者的信念。\n借用原文：the resolution of age-associated disease like heart disease and stroke will inform us\nabout ageing is not based on sound science or logic", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-l-22", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 22, "stem": "Why are managers often frustrated with their employees?", "options": ["A) They are reluctant to follow instructions.", "B) They fail to answer emails promptly.", "C) They cannot understand directives.", "D) They do not show due respect."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） They are reluctant to follow instructions.\n【定位】Much to their frustration, managers often struggle to get their staff to comply with even\nsimple instructions.\n令管理者十分懊恼的是,他们常常难以让员工服从哪怕是简单的指令。\n【信号】frustration 原词点题。这句开头的 Much to their frustration 正是题干 frustrated 的来\n源,紧接着 struggle to get their staff to comply with even simple instructions 直接说出\n管理者头疼的内容——这种与题干同根词后面紧跟的描述句,就是答案句信号。\n【替换】struggle to get their staff to comply with even simple instructions ↔\nreluctant to follow instructions\n服从难=不愿服从\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n选项 fail to answer emails promptly 借了录音后文反复出现的 emails 一词,但管理者懊恼\n的原因是员工不服从指令,不是回邮件不及时,属听到 emails 就想选的原词陷阱。\n借用原文：they're an effective tool for the sharing of data and reports\nC) 未提及\ncannot understand directives 说员工听不懂指令,但录音说的是员工难以被驱使去服从,不\n是看不懂;录音从未提员工理解能力的问题,属过度推断/未提及。\nD) 未提及\ndo not show due respect 说员工不够尊重,录音对员工不服从给的描述是不配合指令,完全\n没谈尊重与否,属无中生有。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-l-23", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 23, "stem": "Why do employees sometimes disobey procedures intentionally?", "options": ["A) They have not been trained to follow the rules.", "B) They are not satisfied with the management.", "C) They want to avoid unnecessary losses.", "D) They find their voice go unheeded."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） They want to avoid unnecessary losses.\n【定位】Occasionally, it's because they're pressured to finish in a short time. At other times, they may\ndisagree with the spirit of the procedure, the effort demanded, the time consumed, the lack of\npotential effectiveness.\n有时,是因为他们被催着要在很短的时间内完成。还有些时候,他们可能不认同这套流程的本\n意——所要付出的精力、所耗费的时间、潜在效果的欠缺。\n【信号】because 引出原因。it's because they're pressured to finish in a short time 用\nbecause 直接给出第一个原因,后面 the effort demanded, the time consumed, the lack\nof potential effectiveness 继续列举,这一连串因果与列举正是答案信号。\n【替换】pressured to finish in a short time ↔ avoid unnecessary losses\n赶时间避免效率损失\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nnot been trained to follow the rules 借了录音里反复出现的 follow/procedures 语境,但\n录音从未说员工没受过训练,只说他们故意不认同流程,属拿原文话题词设的原词陷阱。\n借用原文：how closely they followed any new procedures set by their managers\nB) 偷换概念\nnot satisfied with the management 说员工不满管理层,录音说的是员工不认同流程本身\n(its spirit、effort、time),并非针对管理者本人,把对流程的不认同偷换成对管理层的不满。\n借用原文：they may disagree with the spirit of the procedure\nD) 未提及\nfind their voice go unheeded 说员工意见被忽视,录音这段列违规原因时并未提此点(说话\n受不受重视是第 4、5 段讲的另一回事),属未提及。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-l-24", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 24, "stem": "When are employees more likely to follow instructions, according to the researchers?", "options": ["A) When they are on good terms with their managers.", "B) When they find their job goals easily attainable.", "C) When they find their supervisors helpful.", "D) When they are financially motivated."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） When they find their supervisors helpful.\n【定位】There was a remarkably strong correlation between how helpful supervisors were perceived\nto be and how likely their employees were to follow their directives.\n在上司被认为有多大帮助与员工有多大可能服从其指令之间,存在着极强的相关性。\n【信号】strong correlation 挂钩条件与结果。a remarkably strong correlation between how\nhelpful supervisors were perceived to be and how likely their employees were to\nfollow their directives 用 strong correlation between…and… 把上司有帮助与员工服从\n直接挂钩,这种相关性句式正是条件题的答案信号。\n【替换】how helpful supervisors were perceived to be ↔ find their supervisors helpful\n被认为有帮助=觉得有帮助\n【排除】A) 偷换概念\non good terms with their managers 说与管理者关系好,录音强调的是上司被认为有帮助\n(helpful),关系融洽与提供帮助并非一回事,把 helpful 偷换成私人关系好。\n借用原文：how helpful supervisors were perceived to be\nB) 原词陷阱\nfind their job goals easily attainable 借了录音 the likelihood of success in the\nattainment of job goals 里的 job goals,但录音说的是上司帮助提升了达成目标的可能感,\n目标易达是结果而非员工服从的前提,属盗用原词又因果倒置。\n借用原文：the likelihood of success in the attainment of job goals\nD) 未提及\nfinancially motivated 说靠钱激励,录音讲的激励是上司的帮助行为(helping behavior was\nfound to be motivational),与金钱无关;录音从未提经济激励,属未提及。\n借用原文：Supervisors' helping behavior was found to be motivational in nature", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-l-25", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 25, "stem": "What does the speaker say about emails?", "options": ["A) They are a useless tool for managers to change employee behavior.", "B) They prove to be a good means for managers to give instructions.", "C) They should be reserved for urgent communication.", "D) They are seldom used for sharing confidential data."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （态度观点题） They are a useless tool for managers to change employee behavior.\n【定位】They're an effective tool for the sharing of data and reports, but they're a hopeless tool if what\na manager is desiring is a change in behavior.\n邮件是分享数据和报告的有效工具,但如果管理者想要的是改变行为,它就是个毫无用处的\n工具。\n【信号】but 后才是真立场。but they're a hopeless tool if what a manager is desiring is a\nchange in behavior 中的 but 是关键转折:前半承认邮件传数据有效,but 后说它对改变行为\n毫无用处——转折后那半句才是说话人对邮件的真正定论,是答案信号。\n【替换】they're a hopeless tool if what a manager is desiring is a change in behavior ↔\nThey are a useless tool for managers to change employee behavior.\nhopeless=useless,change behavior 对应\n【排除】B) 与原文相反\na good means for managers to give instructions 对应录音转折前 an effective tool for\nthe sharing of data and reports,但那只是说话人让一步承认的优点,but 后才是真立场;选\nB 等于只听前半截、漏掉转折,属与原文相反。\n借用原文：They're an effective tool for the sharing of data and reports\nC) 未提及\nreserved for urgent communication 说邮件该留给紧急沟通,录音只说邮件适合传数据报\n告、不适合改行为,从未提急事;属无中生有的引申。\nD) 偷换概念\nsharing confidential data 借了录音 the sharing of data and reports 里的 data,却擅自加\n上 confidential(机密),录音只说 data and reports、未限定机密,属盗用原词又加限定的偷\n换。\n借用原文：They're an effective tool for the sharing of data and reports", "points": ["听力·讲座", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) abundance", "B) astonishment", "C) entailed", "D) envisaged", "E) equation", "F) expended", "G) feat", "H) fiscally", "I) impetus", "J) overhaul", "K) permanently", "L) plight", "M) prosper", "N) shatter", "O) ultimately"], "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O) ultimately\n副词\n【词性槽】两个逗号之间的插入语，修饰整句\n【依据】ultimately = 归根结底，与下句\"重量由算术法则决定\"形成\"广告再花哨，最终还得回到算\n术\"的对照\n前半句说 Aisles of marketing genius belie(掩饰) the fact that, __(26)__, weight loss is\ndictated by the laws of arithmetic.——市场营销天才掩盖了一个事实，归根结底，减重\n由算术法则决定。ultimately 完美承接 belie the fact 的转折语气。\n【竞争词】H) fiscally fiscally\"在财务上\"与减肥语境不符\n本句谈 weight loss（减重）即\"减肥\"，fiscally 用于钱的话题；且 fiscally 一般不夹在 the fact\nthat … 句首作副词修饰，不自然。\nK) permanently\npermanently\"永久地\"语义偏离主旨——文章讲的不是\"永久\"减肥而是\"等式\"原理\npermanently 强调持续性，而此处要点是\"最终原理\"不是\"持续时间\"；与下半句\"算术法则\"的归\n结义不匹配。", "points": ["选词填空", "副词"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) abundance", "B) astonishment", "C) entailed", "D) envisaged", "E) equation", "F) expended", "G) feat", "H) fiscally", "I) impetus", "J) overhaul", "K) permanently", "L) plight", "M) prosper", "N) shatter", "O) ultimately"], "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F) expended\n动词\n【词性槽】calories ___，名词+过去分词作后置定语\n【依据】calories expended = 被消耗的卡路里，与 calories taken in 形成\"摄入 vs 消耗\"对照\n原句明显是\"摄入 < 消耗 → 减重\"的算术比较，If calories taken in are less than calories\n__(27)__, weight shall be lost，唯有 expended\"被花费、消耗\"能与 taken in 对仗。这也\n是减肥常识的标准措辞 calories in vs calories out / expended。\n【竞争词】C) entailed entailed\"被牵涉\"与卡路里搭配不通\ncalories entailed 不构成地道短语；entail 多用于\"need/involve\"含义，留给 35 空（required\na paradigm shift and ___ sacrifice and commitment）更合。\nD) envisaged envisaged\"被预想\"与卡路里语义无关\ncalories envisaged 不通；envisage 表设想，留给 31 空（as she had ___ 她原本设想的）\n更贴。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) abundance", "B) astonishment", "C) entailed", "D) envisaged", "E) equation", "F) expended", "G) feat", "H) fiscally", "I) impetus", "J) overhaul", "K) permanently", "L) plight", "M) prosper", "N) shatter", "O) ultimately"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) abundance\n名词\n【词性槽】the + 空 + of 结构必填名词\n【依据】an abundance of … 是固定搭配，且\"丰富的金融产品\"与下文\"reduction of\nspending\"形成让步对比\n原句以 Despite 引导让步：Despite the __(28)__ of financial products, services and\nsolutions geared towards accumulating wealth, it all begins with the same __(29)__\n——尽管金融产品\"丰富\"，开头仍是同一套基本原理。an abundance of 是地道搭配，语义\n指向\"大量、丰富\"，与转折后\"回归本源\"形成对照，故选 abundance。\n【竞争词】B) astonishment 搭配错配——常用 to one's astonishment，不与 of 引出大量事物\nastonishment 表\"惊讶\"，是情绪名词，不与 \"of financial products\" 这种\"大量事物\"搭配；且\n语义与 Despite 让步逻辑无关。\nE) equation 需用在 29 空指\"基本等式/原理\"，且不与 of products 搭\nequation 留给 29 空（the same __(29)__ 指共同等式）更贴；此处\"产品的等式\"语义不通。\nG) feat feat 指单一壮举，不能用 of 引出大量并列名词\nfeat 表\"一项了不起的成就\"，通常说 a feat of strength/engineering，不说 the feat of\nproducts, services and solutions。\nI) impetus impetus 是\"推动力\"，不与\"产品、服务、方案\"搭\nimpetus 常说 an impetus to/for sth.（对某事的推力），与 of 引出的物品并列不通。\nL) plight plight 是\"困境\"，与\"积累财富的产品\"语义反向\nplight 留给 33 空（financial plight 财务困境）更贴；此处与 Despite 让步逻辑、与\"产品丰\n富\"语境完全不合。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) abundance", "B) astonishment", "C) entailed", "D) envisaged", "E) equation", "F) expended", "G) feat", "H) fiscally", "I) impetus", "J) overhaul", "K) permanently", "L) plight", "M) prosper", "N) shatter", "O) ultimately"], "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E) equation\n名词\n【词性槽】the same + 空，限定词后填可数名词单数\n【依据】冒号后给出的\"收入 > 支出\"正是一道等式，呼应首段\"weight loss is dictated by the\nlaws of arithmetic\"\n首段 weight loss is dictated by the laws of arithmetic 与 If calories taken in are less\nthan calories __(27)__, weight shall be lost, and so it is with money. 已用\"算术等式\"定\n调；29 空冒号后 income is greater than expenses 正是同一道\"等式\"，所以 the same\nequation 与全文比喻一脉相承。\n【竞争词】G) feat feat 是\"壮举\"，不是\"原理\"\nthe same feat\"同一项壮举\"与冒号后\"收入大于支出\"这种基本法则不对应，feat 留给 30 空。\nI) impetus impetus 是\"动力\"而非\"等式\"\nthe same impetus 读作\"同一种推动力\"，与下文给出的具体收支关系式不匹配。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) abundance", "B) astonishment", "C) entailed", "D) envisaged", "E) equation", "F) expended", "G) feat", "H) fiscally", "I) impetus", "J) overhaul", "K) permanently", "L) plight", "M) prosper", "N) shatter", "O) ultimately"], "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G) feat\n名词\n【词性槽】quite a + 空，单数可数名词\n【依据】quite a feat = 真是了不起的壮举，对应 Nicole 两年内从负债到买房\n上句 explaining how she cracked the property market at 31——31 岁就攻克房地产市\n场，再回看两年前的窘境，称之\"壮举\"恰到好处；quite a feat 是高频地道表达。\n【竞争词】I) impetus impetus 不是评价性\"成就\"名词\nquite an impetus 几乎不用，且 impetus 表\"动力\"，与\"两年前一无所有 → 现在买房\"这种结果\n性评价不对路。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) abundance", "B) astonishment", "C) entailed", "D) envisaged", "E) equation", "F) expended", "G) feat", "H) fiscally", "I) impetus", "J) overhaul", "K) permanently", "L) plight", "M) prosper", "N) shatter", "O) ultimately"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D) envisaged\n动词\n【词性槽】had + 空，现在完成时（过去完成时）需过去分词\n【依据】as she had envisaged = 如她原本设想的那样，与下文\"实际却在哭\"形成反差\nas she had envisaged 是典型的\"contrary to what she had envisaged\"句式：Nicole 原\n本设想 30 岁生日会怎样庆祝，结果 She was sobbing at the dinner table with her\nparents, with whom she had just moved back in.——现实与设想反向，envisaged 完美\n承接。\n【竞争词】C) entailed entailed 主语不应是\"她\"，且语义\"牵涉\"与\"庆祝生日\"反向不通\nas she had entailed\"如她所牵涉的\"读不通；entail 主语通常是事/物，不是人。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) abundance", "B) astonishment", "C) entailed", "D) envisaged", "E) equation", "F) expended", "G) feat", "H) fiscally", "I) impetus", "J) overhaul", "K) permanently", "L) plight", "M) prosper", "N) shatter", "O) ultimately"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B) astonishment\n名词\n【词性槽】but to her ___ 句式，介词 to + 物主代词 + 名词\n【依据】to her astonishment 套语 + 与 sobbing → 父亲安慰形成意外反差\n她哭得伤心，预期父亲会同样悲观，但父亲却说 her financial __(33)__ wasn't as bad as\nshe thought——这恰是\"令她惊讶\"的反差，astonishment 完美承接。\n【竞争词】E) equation to her equation 不成立\nequation 不是情绪名词，不入 to one's ___ 套语。\nG) feat feat 留给 30 空（quite a feat 壮举）\nto her feat 不通；feat 是成就名词，非情绪反应。\nI) impetus to her impetus 不成立\nimpetus 表\"动力\"，与 to one's ___ 套语不搭，也与上下文反差逻辑无关。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) abundance", "B) astonishment", "C) entailed", "D) envisaged", "E) equation", "F) expended", "G) feat", "H) fiscally", "I) impetus", "J) overhaul", "K) permanently", "L) plight", "M) prosper", "N) shatter", "O) ultimately"], "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L) plight\n名词\n【词性槽】形容词 financial + 空，需名词被修饰\n【依据】financial plight = 财务困境，与上文\"没有稳定收入 + 1.2 万信用卡债\"对应\n上文交代 She had no stable income, $12,000 in credit-card debt and no plan——典\n型的财务\"困境\"；父亲安慰说 financial plight\"不像她想的那么糟\"，语义、逻辑、搭配三\n合一。\n【竞争词】B) astonishment financial astonishment 不成搭配\nastonishment 是情绪词，不与 financial 搭配；且要给 32 空（to her astonishment）保留。\nE) equation equation 给 29 空更贴，且\"财务等式不像那么糟\"语义不通\n财务等式无所谓\"糟不糟\"；equation 指向\"原理/等式\"，与 wasn't as bad 不配。\nG) feat feat 是褒义\"壮举\"，与 wasn't as bad as 反向\n壮举不会\"不那么糟\"；语义色彩冲突。\nI) impetus impetus 表\"动力\"，与\"糟糕\"语义不匹配\nfinancial impetus 不是地道搭配，且\"动力不像那么糟\"读不通。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 E) —— financial equation 看似也通，但留给 29 更贴。考生可能\n把 equation 填到 33 空——\"她的财务等式不像那么糟\"——但 equation 是 29 空的命门\n（冒号定义=收入>支出）。识破：用过即划——若把 equation 放 33，29 就找不到同等\n贴切的名词。防坑：碰到\"两个空都像\"的同词性词，先看哪个空的搭配更稀缺/更被锁死，把\n词留给那个空。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) abundance", "B) astonishment", "C) entailed", "D) envisaged", "E) equation", "F) expended", "G) feat", "H) fiscally", "I) impetus", "J) overhaul", "K) permanently", "L) plight", "M) prosper", "N) shatter", "O) ultimately"], "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J) overhaul\n动词\n【词性槽】to + 空 + 宾语，不定式后的动词原形\n【依据】overhaul her spending = 彻底整顿她的开支，与\"回归正轨\"目的句呼应\n原句目的 to get herself on track financially——为了在财务上回到正轨，需要的正是\"对\n开支（和生活）彻底整顿\"。overhaul 既可及物且常与 spending、system 等大范围对象搭\n配，最贴。\n【竞争词】M) prosper prosper 是不及物动词，且词义\"繁荣\"不与 spending 搭\nprosper her spending 在语法和搭配上都不成立——一是 prosper 不带宾语，二是\"使开支繁\n荣\"与节流目的相反。\nN) shatter shatter 是\"粉碎\"，色彩过激且与上下文不符\nshatter her spending\"粉碎开支\"不是地道说法；shatter 通常对应玻璃、希望、记录等抽象/具\n体对象，且色彩破坏性过强。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 M) —— prosper 看似\"使财务繁荣\"，但忽略了它不及物 + 与节流\n相反。__(34)__ her spending 中 spending 是宾语，prosper 不及物即出局；且文章主旨\n是\"减少开支\"非\"放大开支\"。防坑：动词候选先做两件事——查及物性、对照目的句方向。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) abundance", "B) astonishment", "C) entailed", "D) envisaged", "E) equation", "F) expended", "G) feat", "H) fiscally", "I) impetus", "J) overhaul", "K) permanently", "L) plight", "M) prosper", "N) shatter", "O) ultimately"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C) entailed\n动词\n【词性槽】required … and ___，与 required 并列的过去式动词\n【依据】entailed sacrifice and commitment = 必然要求/牵涉牺牲和承诺\nCreating a gap … required a paradigm shift and entailed sacrifice and commitment\n——\"创造收支差距既需要思路转变、也必然牵涉牺牲和承诺\"，entail 与 require 同义并\n列，是地道搭配。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "The total amount of damage to Notre Dame Cathedral can be assessed only when its structure is considered safe.", "options": null, "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】结构安全才能评估整体损失\n【定位】However, fire officers have said a complete inventory of the damage will not be possible until\nthe cathedral structure has been deemed safe.\n然而，消防官员表示，只有等大教堂的结构被认定为安全后，才有可能对损失进行完整的\n清点。\n【改写】The total amount of damage ↔ a complete inventory of the damage\n整体名词化改写\ncan be assessed only when its structure is considered safe ↔\nwill not be possible until the cathedral structure has been deemed safe\nonly when ↔ not...until 条件等值\n【辨邻】 H H 段虽提到 Police and fire services will spend the next 48 hours assessing the\n\"security and safety\" of the 850-year-old structure，但讲的是评估建筑物的安全\n性、给出未来 48 小时的具体时长，并未把完整损失清点的可能性与结构安全挂钩，与\n题干说的 完整损失清点的前提条件 不是一回事。\nPolice and fire services will spend the next 48 hours assessing the \"security and safety\" of the 850-\nyear-old structure", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "Once again people began to argue whether Notre Dame Cathedral was going to collapse even without the fire.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】再次开始争论\n【定位】But as the emergency services picked through the burnt debris, a row was resurfacing over\naccusations that the beloved cathedral, immortalised in Victor Hugo's novel, was already\ncrumbling before the fire.\n但当应急部门在焚毁的废墟中翻找时，一场争论重新浮出水面：有人指责这座因雨果小说而\n流芳百世的心爱大教堂，在大火之前就已经在崩塌了。\n【改写】Once again people began to argue ↔ a row was resurfacing\n动词短语 ↔ 名词化 + 同义动词替换\nwhether Notre Dame Cathedral was going to collapse even without the fire ↔\nthe beloved cathedral, immortalised in Victor Hugo's novel, was already crumbling before the\nfire\ngoing to collapse ↔ already crumbling 等值\n【辨邻】 D D 段确实讲了 a years-long dispute over who should finance restoration work of\nthe collapsing staircases, crumbling statues and cracked walls，但这是多年来谁\n出钱修缮的纠纷，且是一直存在的状态，没有 重新浮现 这层意思；与题干 Once\nagain...began to argue 这个 再次开始 的动态信号对不上。\nhas been at the centre of a years-long dispute over who should finance restoration work of the\ncollapsing staircases, crumbling statues and cracked walls\nK K 段说 criticism over the original state of the building is likely to intensify over\ncoming days，强调的是 未来几天批评将加剧，是强度的提升，不是 Once\nagain...began to argue 所要求的 争论再次开始这个动作。\ncriticism over the original state of the building is likely to intensify over coming days", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "The Notre Dame Cathedral catastrophe was said to have helped unite the French nation.", "options": null, "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】灾难帮助团结法国\n【定位】French political commentators noted the devastating fire had succeeded where Macron had\nfailed in uniting the country.\n法国政治评论员指出，这场毁灭性的大火在团结国家方面做到了马克龙未能做到的事。\n【改写】The Notre Dame Cathedral catastrophe ↔ the devastating fire\n上位词替换\nwas said to have helped unite the French nation ↔\nhad succeeded where Macron had failed in uniting the country\nsucceeded in ↔ helped to 等值 + 客观叙述 ↔ was said to\n【辨邻】 E E 段虽有 It's the cathedral of every French person, even those who have never\nvisited it 之类的全民情感表述，但那是马克龙的抒情讲话，强调全民共有的文化记\n忆，并未提出 大火让全国团结 这个动作；与题干的 helped unite the French nation\n在落点上不一样。\nIt's the cathedral of every French person, even those who have never visited it", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "The roof of Notre Dame Cathedral was built with large numbers of densely laid-out wood beams.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】屋顶密铺木梁\n【定位】The fire, which had started at the base of the 93-metre spire(尖塔) at about 6:40 pm on\nMonday, spread through the cathedral's roof, made up of hundreds of oak beams, some dating\nback to the 13th century. These beams, known as la forêt (the forest) because of their density,\nformed the cross-shaped roof that ran the length of the central part of the cathedral.\n大火于周一晚上约 6 点 40 分从 93 米高尖塔的底部燃起，蔓延至大教堂的屋顶——屋顶由\n数百根橡木横梁构成，其中一些可追溯到 13 世纪。由于密度极高，这些横梁被称为 la\nforêt（森林），构成了延伸至大教堂中央部分整个长度的十字形屋顶。\n【改写】The roof of Notre Dame Cathedral ↔ the cathedral's roof\n几乎逐词对应\nlarge numbers of densely laid-out wood beams ↔ hundreds of oak beams\n数量短语等值 + 上位词改写\ndensely laid-out ↔ because of their density\n副词 ↔ 名词同根词改写\n【辨邻】 H H 段提到 If the wooden frame of the towers had caught fire 与 Most of the\nwooden roof beams have been burned，前者讲钟楼木架，后者讲屋顶横梁已被烧\n毁的后果；与题干 屋顶由大量密铺的木梁构成 这一构造描述不是同一回事。\nMost of the wooden roof beams have been burned, and parts of the concrete holding up the roof\nhave collapsed", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "Renovation workers of Notre Dame Cathedral were questioned to find out the cause of the accident.", "options": null, "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】讯问修缮工人查火因\n【定位】The revelation of how close France came to losing its most famous cathedral emerged as\npolice investigators questioned workers involved in the restoration of the monument to try to\nestablish the cause of the devastating blaze.\n在警方调查人员讯问参与古迹修缮的工人以试图查明这场毁灭性大火起因的同时，法国与失\n去其最著名大教堂的距离之近也被披露了出来。\n【改写】Renovation workers of Notre Dame Cathedral ↔\nworkers involved in the restoration of the monument\n同义词替换 + 表达结构互换\nwere questioned to find out the cause of the accident ↔\npolice investigators questioned workers...to try to establish the cause of the devastating blaze\n主被动转换 + 同义动词与名词替换\n【辨邻】 K K 段说 The Paris prosecutor's office has opened an inquiry into \"involuntary\ndestruction by fire\"，强调的是 检察官办公室就 过失纵火 立案调查，并未讲 讯问工\n人 这个具体动作，与题干 Renovation workers...were questioned 不是同一回事。\nThe Paris prosecutor's office has opened an inquiry into \"involuntary destruction by fire\", indicating\nthey believe the cause of the blaze was accidental rather than criminal", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "Had the bell towers' wooden frames burned down, the heavy bells would have crashed down.", "options": null, "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】钟楼木架若烧大钟将砸下（虚拟语气）\n【定位】If the wooden frame of the towers had caught fire, it could have sent the bells—the largest of\nwhich, the Emmanuel Bell, weighs 13 tons—crashing down, potentially causing the collapse\nof both towers.\n如果钟楼的木结构起火，可能会让那些钟——其中最大的 Emmanuel 大钟重达 13 吨——\n砸落下来，可能导致两座钟楼一起倒塌。\n【改写】Had the bell towers' wooden frames burned down ↔\nIf the wooden frame of the towers had caught fire\n虚拟语气倒装与正常语序等值\nthe heavy bells would have crashed down ↔\nit could have sent the bells—the largest of which, the Emmanuel Bell, weighs 13 tons—\ncrashing down\n虚拟语气结果分句等值 + heavy 概括 13 tons\n【辨邻】 A A 段讲的是 firefighters battled to stop flames reaching its bell towers 与 to\ncreate a wall of water between the raging fire and the two towers，是实际发生\n的阻止动作，而非题干 假如木架烧了大钟会砸下 这种虚拟语气下的反事实推断。\nfirefighters battled to stop flames reaching its bell towers on Monday evening", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "The timely action of the firefighters prevented the fire from reaching the Cathedral's bell towers.", "options": null, "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】消防员及时行动护钟楼\n【定位】Notre Dame Cathedral in the heart of Paris was within \"15 to 30 minutes\" of complete\ndestruction as firefighters battled to stop flames reaching its bell towers on Monday evening,\nFrench authorities have revealed.\n法国当局披露，周一晚上巴黎市中心的圣母院在距离被彻底烧毁还差 15 到 30 分钟时，消\n防员还在奋力阻止火焰蔓延至钟楼。\n【改写】The timely action of the firefighters ↔\nwas within \"15 to 30 minutes\" of complete destruction as firefighters battled\n抽象 timely ↔ 具体 within 15-30 minutes 等值\nprevented the fire from reaching the Cathedral's bell towers ↔\nstop flames reaching its bell towers\nprevent from doing ↔ stop doing 同义\n【辨邻】 H H 段确实有 battled to prevent the flames from reaching the two main towers，\n但整段重心是 钟楼木架若起火大钟将砸下 与 三处主要破损评估，并未围绕 及时性\n（timely action）展开；题干强调的 timely 在 A 段以 15-30 分钟 这一具体时间窗集\n中呈现。\nthe 500 firefighters at the scene then battled to prevent the flames from reaching the two main\ntowers, where the cathedral bells hang", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "Apart from the fire, the water used to extinguish it also caused a lot of damage to Notre Dame Cathedral.", "options": null, "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】灭火用水也造成损害\n【定位】As well as damage from the heat, which firefighters said reached more than 800 ℃, experts\nalso need to assess damage from the vast quantities of water firefighters poured into the\ncathedral. One casualty of this was The Great Organ constructed in the 1730s, which was said\nto have escaped the flames but been significantly damaged by water.\n除了消防员所说超过 800 摄氏度高温造成的损坏外，专家还需评估消防员灌入大教堂的大量\n水所造成的损坏。其中一个受害者就是建于 1730 年代的大管风琴——据说它躲过了火焰，\n却被水严重损坏。\n【改写】Apart from the fire ↔ As well as damage from the heat\nApart from ↔ As well as 同义结构\nthe water used to extinguish it also caused a lot of damage to Notre Dame Cathedral ↔\nexperts also need to assess damage from the vast quantities of water firefighters poured into\nthe cathedral\n灭火用水的两种描述等值\n【辨邻】 I I 段虽出现 large pools of grey water from the fire hoses，但这只是描述地面状况\n的细节，并未把 灭火用水 单独列为损害源；题干强调 灭火用水也造成大量损害 这一\n独立损害源的判断，I 段没有。\nAsh covered the marble diamond-patterned floor and floated in large pools of grey water from the\nfire hoses", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "There has been argument over the years as to who should pay for the restoration of Notre Dame Cathedral.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】多年来争论谁出钱修缮\n【定位】The cathedral is owned by the French state and has been at the centre of a years-long dispute\nover who should finance restoration work of the collapsing staircases, crumbling statues and\ncracked walls.\n这座大教堂由法国国家所有，多年来一直处于一场长期纠纷的中心：到底应由谁来出资修缮\n其坍塌的楼梯、剥落的雕像和裂缝的墙体。\n【改写】There has been argument over the years ↔ has been at the centre of a years-long dispute\n同义名词 + 时间表达等值\nas to who should pay for the restoration of Notre Dame Cathedral ↔\nover who should finance restoration work\n动词 finance ↔ pay for 同义 + 介词等值\n【辨邻】 C C 段讲的是 French business leaders and global corporations announced they\nwould donate to a restoration campaign launched by the president，是 周二几\n个小时内承诺捐款 这种 即时、新发生 的事件；与题干 多年来争论 在时间属性上完全\n不同。\nFrench business leaders and global corporations announced they would donate to a restoration\ncampaign launched by the president, Emmanuel Macron", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "News of the Notre Dame Cathedral catastrophe instantly caught media attention throughout the world.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】灾难新闻瞬间引起全球媒体关注\n【定位】Indeed, within minutes the fire occupied headlines of every major global newspaper and\ntelevision network.\n事实上，几分钟之内，这场大火就占据了全球各大报纸和电视网的头条。\n【改写】News of the Notre Dame Cathedral catastrophe ↔ the fire\n事件 ↔ 事件本身的同义指代\ninstantly caught media attention throughout the world ↔\nwithin minutes the fire occupied headlines of every major global newspaper and television\nnetwork\n副词 ↔ 时间窗 + 抽象 ↔ 具体改写", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "What do people generally think about drawing?", "options": ["A) It is a gift creative people are endowed with.", "B) It is a skill that is acquired with practice.", "C) It is an art form that is appreciated by all.", "D) It is an ability everyone should cultivate."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What do people generally think about drawing? 问的是大众对绘画的普遍认知，属于事\n实型细节题。\n【拆句】What do people generally think about drawing? 是简单的 what 引导特殊疑问句，主语\npeople，谓语 think，宾语为 what，句式直白、无陷阱。\n【定位】We often think of drawing as something that takes inborn talent, but this kind of thinking\nstems from our misclassification of drawing as, primarily, an art form rather than a tool\nfor learning.\n我们常把绘画看作需要天生才能的事情，但这种想法源于我们把绘画错误归类——主要把它\n当作艺术形式而非学习工具。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nsomething that takes inborn talent ↔ a gift creative people are endowed with\n预判的\"天生才能\"与 a gift creative people are endowed with 一一对应，A 项是定位\n句前半的精准同义替换。\nB) 无中生有\nIt is a skill that is acquired with practice. 称绘画是通过练习习得的技能，但 P1 强调\n的恰恰是\"天生才能\"而非\"练习习得\"，文中没有任何句子说大众认为绘画靠练习。\nC) 偷换概念\nIt is an art form that is appreciated by all. 称绘画是被所有人欣赏的艺术形式，但\nour misclassification of drawing as, primarily, an art form 中作者只是引用大众把它\n归为艺术形式这一错误归类，并未谈及\"被所有人欣赏\"。\nD) 张冠李戴\nIt is an ability everyone should cultivate. 称绘画是人人都应培养的能力，这是作者在\nwe should consider it as a tool for seeing and understanding the world better 中\n的呼吁，是作者立场而非大众看法。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "What do we learn about designers and artists?", "options": ["A) They are declining gradually in number.", "B) They are keen on changing shabby surroundings.", "C) They add beauty and charm to the world.", "D) They spend most of their lives drawing."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What do we learn about designers and artists? 问的是我们对这群人的事实性认识。\n【拆句】What do we learn about designers and artists? 主谓宾结构 we learn what about X，\n关于 X 我们学到了什么，句式平直无陷阱。\n【定位】There are people who don't, obviously, and thank God for that: a world without designers and\nartists would be a very shabby one indeed.\n显然有人没有放弃画画，谢天谢地：没有设计师和艺术家的世界，将会是一个非常单调寒酸\n的世界。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nThey are declining gradually in number. 称他们数量在逐渐减少，但 P3 只说\"多数人\n长大后放弃画画、但有人没放弃\"，未提\"设计师和艺术家\"的数量趋势。\nB) 偷换概念\nThey are keen on changing shabby surroundings. 称他们热衷于改变 shabby 的环\n境，但原文 shabby 是用来形容\"如果没有他们，世界会怎样\"，是一个假设性结果，而\n非他们当下要改变的对象。\nC) ✓ 正确\na world without designers and artists would be a very shabby one indeed ↔\nThey add beauty and charm to the world.\n把\"没有他们世界会 shabby\"还原为\"他们让世界不 shabby、即美丽迷人\"，正好对应\nThey add beauty and charm to the world.。\nD) 以偏概全\nThey spend most of their lives drawing. 称他们一生大部分时间都在画画，但 P3 只\n说他们没有像普通人那样停止画画，并未说他们\"一生大部分时间都在画画\"。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "What does Professor D. B. Dowd argue in his book?", "options": ["A) Everybody is born with the capacity to draw.", "B) Drawing is a skill that requires special training.", "C) The value of drawing tends to be overestimated.", "D) Drawing should be redefined as a realistic illusion."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What does Professor D. B. Dowd argue in his book? 问具体某人在其书中提出的论点，\n属事实型细节题。\n【拆句】What does Professor D. B. Dowd argue in his book? 是 What 引导的特殊疑问句，\nargue 为关键动词，地点状语 in his book 仅作辅助定位，结构平直。\n【定位】In his book, Stick Figures: Drawing as a Human Practice, Professor D. B. Dowd argues that\nwe have misfiled the significance of drawing because we see it as a professional skill instead\nof a personal capacity.\n在《火柴人：作为人类实践的绘画》一书中，D. B. Dowd 教授认为，我们把绘画的意义错\n误归类了，因为我们把它视为一种专业技能，而非一种个人能力。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\na personal capacity ↔ Everybody is born with the capacity to draw.\ninstead of a personal capacity ↔ Everybody is born with the capacity to draw.\n教授的真正主张是 instead of 后的 a personal capacity（个人能力），其语义内核\n即\"人人都具备\"——正好对应 Everybody is born with the capacity to draw.。\nB) 与原文相反\nDrawing is a skill that requires special training. 称绘画是需要专门训练的技能，但\nwe see it as a professional skill instead of a personal capacity 中\"专业技能\"恰恰是\n教授要反对的错误归类，B 选项与教授立场南辕北辙。\nC) 与原文相反\nThe value of drawing tends to be overestimated. 称绘画的价值常被高估，但 we\nhave misfiled the significance of drawing 中教授说的是\"被错误归类\"而非\"被高估\"，\n且全文基调是\"绘画的价值被低估\"——教授要为绘画正名。\nD) 与原文相反\nDrawing should be redefined as a realistic illusion. 称绘画应被重新定义为\"真实幻\n象\"，但 Rather, drawing should be recategorized as a symbolic tool 明确说应被重\n新归类为\"符号化工具\"；而 realistic illusion 是教授要反对的旧定义。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "What have some researchers found from one study about doodling?", "options": ["A) It is a must for maintaining a base level of brain activity.", "B) It is the most reliable stimulant to activate the brain.", "C) It can turn something boring into something interesting.", "D) It helps improve concentration and memory."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What have some researchers found from one study about doodling? 问研究人员从一\n项具体研究中发现了什么，是事实细节。\n【拆句】What have some researchers found from one study about doodling? 主干\nresearchers have found what，状语 from one study 与 about doodling 双重限定研究\n对象，句式紧凑无陷阱。\n【定位】Because of this, some believe that doodling during a boring lecture can help students pay\nattention. In one study, participants were asked to listen to a list of names while either\ndoodling or sitting still. Those who doodled remembered 29 percent more of the names than\nthose who did not.\n正因如此，有人认为在乏味的讲座中涂画可以帮助学生集中注意力。在一项研究中，参与者\n被要求一边听一串名字一边或涂画或静坐。涂画者比不涂画者多记住 29% 的名字。\n【选项】A) 以偏概全\nIt is a must for maintaining a base level of brain activity. 称涂画是维持大脑基线活\n动的必要条件（a must），但 doodling activates the brain's so-called default\ncircuit 只说涂画\"激活\"默认回路，并未说它是\"必需的\"，且默认回路本身就是在缺乏其\n他刺激时维持基线活动，不依赖涂画。\nB) 无中生有\nIt is the most reliable stimulant to activate the brain. 称涂画是激活大脑最可靠的刺\n激物，P6 只说\"有些研究者认为\"涂画能激活默认回路，从未将其与其他刺激物比较或\n评级。\nC) 偷换概念\nIt can turn something boring into something interesting. 称涂画能化无聊为有趣，\n但 doodling during a boring lecture can help students pay attention 只说在乏\n味讲座中涂画能助你专注，并未说讲座因此变有趣——是\"帮你听下去\"，不是\"让它变\n好玩\"。\nD) ✓ 正确\ncan help students pay attention ↔ It helps improve concentration and memory.\nThose who doodled remembered 29 percent more of the names than those who did not ↔\nIt helps improve concentration and memory.\nD 的两个并列成分 concentration / memory 各自由原文两句独立支撑（pay attention\n/ remembered more），实现完整覆盖。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What is characteristic of people with drawing talent?", "options": ["A) Sensitivity to cognitive stimulation.", "B) Subtlety of representation.", "C) Accuracy in categorization.", "D) Precision in visual perception."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What is characteristic of people with drawing talent? 问的是有绘画天赋的人具有什么特\n征，原文必有明示。\n【拆句】What is characteristic of people with drawing talent? 主干 what is characteristic of\nX，characteristic 在此为形容词，介词 of 引出 X，句式简洁无陷阱。\n【定位】There's also evidence that drawing talent is based on how accurately someone perceives the\nworld. The human visual system tends to misjudge size, shape, color, and angles but artists\nperceive these qualities more accurately than non-artists.\n也有证据表明，绘画才能取决于一个人感知世界的准确程度。人类视觉系统常误判大小、形\n状、颜色和角度，但艺术家能比非艺术家更准确地感知这些特性。\n【选项】A) 张冠李戴\nSensitivity to cognitive stimulation. 称对认知刺激敏感，但 P7 谈的是视觉感知\n（visual perception）的准确度，并未涉及对认知刺激（cognitive stimulation）的敏\n感度——cognitive stimulation 是来自第 6 段涂画/默认回路话题的措辞。\nB) 与原文相反\nSubtlety of representation. 称表现手法精巧，但 We mistakenly think of \"good\"\ndrawings as those which work as recreations of the real world, as realistic\nillusions 明确表示\"把绘画当作现实再现\"是错误归类；且本题问的是绘画者的特征而非\n作品的特征。\nC) 偷换概念\nAccuracy in categorization. 称分类的准确性，但 P7 说的是感知（perception）的准\n确性，并非分类（categorization）能力；文章中 categorization 一词只出现在第 4 段\n讨论绘画的\"归类\"问题，与艺术家的视觉能力无关。\nD) ✓ 正确\nartists perceive these qualities more accurately than non-artists ↔\nPrecision in visual perception.\nThe human visual system tends to misjudge size, shape, color, and angles ↔\nPrecision in visual perception.\n选项 D 把原文\"perceive more accurately\"整体名词化为\"precision in visual\nperception\"，是定位句的最简同义替换。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What does the author say about car advertisements?", "options": ["A) They portray drivers who enjoy speed on the road.", "B) They present a false picture of the autonomy cars provide.", "C) They pursue individuality and originality in design concept.", "D) They overestimate the potential market of autonomous cars."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author say about car advertisements 直接问作者对广告的具体表述，无\ninfer/imply 等推断信号，属对原文某句的客观转述。\n【拆句】标准 What does the author say about X 句式，宾语从句结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】There is something almost delightful in the detachment from reality of advertisements\nshowing mass-produced cars marketed as symbols of individuality and of freedom when most\nof their lives will be spent making short journeys on choked roads.\n广告中那种\"脱离现实\"的味道几乎让人觉得有趣——量产车被包装成个性与自由的象征，可\n它们这辈子大多数时间都堵在短途的拥挤路上。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\n原文谈的是个性 / 自由的形象与拥堵现实的反差，没有任何句子说广告里的司机享受\n速度。\nB) ✓ 正确\ndetachment from reality of advertisements showing mass-produced cars marketed as symbols of\nindividuality and of freedom\n↔ They present a false picture of the autonomy cars provide\n预判要的\"广告呈现的自由是失真的\"，B 恰好以 false picture + autonomy 两词整齐对\n应原文，是定位句的同义压缩。\nC) 偷换概念\n原文说广告把车包装为\"个性的象征\"（marketed as symbols of individuality），不是\n说广告在\"设计理念上追求个性与原创\"。\nD) 张冠李戴\n原文谈的是普通量产车广告，autonomous cars（自动驾驶车）要到 P5 才出现，且与\n广告无关。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "What does the author imply about the various gadgets on cars?", "options": ["A) They can help to alleviate traffic jams.", "B) Most of them are as effective as advertised.", "C) Only some can be put to use under current traffic conditions.", "D) They are constantly upgraded to make driving easier and safer."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （推断题）\n【判型】What does the author imply about the various gadgets on cars 中 imply 是典型推断\n信号，要求在原文未明说的方向上做一步延伸。\n【拆句】What does the author imply about X 标准句式，结构无难度，关键是认出 imply 的题型\n信号。\n【定位】For all the fuss made about top speeds, cornering ability and acceleration, the most useful\ngadgets on a modern car are those which work when you're going very slowly: parking\nsensors, sound systems, and navigation apps which will show a way around upcoming\ntraffic jams.\n尽管人们对极速、过弯和加速大谈特谈，但现代车上真正有用的小装置，反而都是在你开得\n很慢时才派上用场的那些——停车感应、音响、导航。\n【选项】A) 过度推断\n原文说导航 apps 能 spot 拥堵并 suggest ways round，但下一段立刻指出这只是把堵\n车\"转移\"到 side roads，并未真正缓解（alleviate）。\nB) 无中生有\n原文只罗列 gadgets 哪些有用，并没有把它们与广告所宣传的效果做比较，无从得\n出\"多数与广告一样有效\"。\nC) ✓ 正确\nthe most useful gadgets on a modern car are those which work when you're going very slowly\n↔ Only some can be put to use under current traffic conditions\n预判要的\"只有一部分装置真正派用\"，C 用 only some 精准对上原文 those which... 的\n子集限定，是唯一吻合预判的选项。\nD) 无中生有\n原文没有讨论 gadgets \"不断升级\"，更未涉及\"让驾驶更轻松/安全\"的整体趋势。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "推断题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What does the author say about the use of navigation apps?", "options": ["A) It is likely to create traffic jams in other places.", "B) It helps a great deal in easing traffic congestion.", "C) It sharply reduces the incidence of traffic accidents.", "D) It benefits those who are learning to drive."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author say about the use of navigation apps 用 say 而非 imply，属客\n观转述类细节题。\n【拆句】What does the author say about the use of X 标准句式，宾语是动名词短语 the use of\nnavigation apps，平铺无陷阱。\n【定位】But when everyone has perfect information, traffic jams simply spread onto the side roads\nthat seem to offer a way round them.\n但当所有人都掌握完美信息时，堵车只会蔓延到那些看起来能绕开拥堵的次要道路上去。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\ntraffic jams simply spread onto the side roads that seem to offer a way round them ↔\nIt is likely to create traffic jams in other places\n预判要的\"导航把堵车推到别处\"，A 用 create traffic jams + in other places 精准对应\nspread onto + the side roads，是定位句的同义改写。\nB) 与原文相反\n原文核心是导航 apps 只能把堵车搬走、不能从总量上 ease it；选项却说\"对缓解拥堵帮\n助巨大\"，与作者立场相反。\nC) 无中生有\n原文全段讨论的是\"避堵\"，从未涉及\"交通事故发生率\"，更没说能 sharply reduce。\nD) 偷换概念\n原文未提\"学车人\"这一群体，只笼统说 everyone / a lucky few；选项偷换为\"对学车者\n有益\"。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "What does the author say about technology?", "options": ["A) Its consequences are usually difficult to assess.", "B) It seldom delivers all the benefits as promised.", "C) It depends on the required knowledge for application.", "D) Its benefits are guaranteed by collective wisdom."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author say about technology 仍是 say 句式，要求转述作者对\ntechnology 的明确表述。\n【拆句】What does the author say about technology 标准句式，结构简单；难点不在题干而在\ntechnology 在原文的复现密度——需靠 byWords 中的\"评价句\"通法收敛。\n【定位】The first is that the promises of technology will never be realised as fully as we hope; they\nwill be limited by their unforeseen and unintended consequences.\n第一是，技术的种种承诺永远无法像我们期望的那样被完全兑现；它们总会被那些未曾预\n料、并非本意的后果所制约。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\n原文说的是\"后果未被预见 / 并非本意\"（unforeseen and unintended），而非\"难以评\n估\"（difficult to assess）。\nB) ✓ 正确\nthe promises of technology will never be realised as fully as we hope ↔\nIt seldom delivers all the benefits as promised\n预判要的\"技术承诺难以完全兑现\"，B 用 seldom + all ... as promised 精准对应原文的\nnever + as fully as we hope。\nC) 无中生有\n原文未讨论\"技术应用是否依赖知识储备\"。\nD) 与原文相反\n原文末句明说 even that much is never guaranteed（即使集中管控也无法保证），选\n项却说\"集体智慧保证了技术的好处\"，与作者立场相反。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-2-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What key message does the author try to convey in the passage?", "options": ["A) The consequences of technological innovation need not be exaggerated.", "B) There is always a price to pay to develop technology for a better world.", "C) Technological innovation should be properly regulated.", "D) The culture of Silicon Valley ought not to be emulated."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （主旨题）\n【判型】What key message does the author try to convey in the passage 中 key message +\nin the passage 是典型主旨题双信号。\n【拆句】What key message does the author try to convey in the passage——疑问句倒装，主\n语 the author，谓语 try to convey，宾语 what key message，结构清楚不绕。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\n全文核心是要正视技术后果、加以监管，而非\"不必夸大后果\"——选项的方向与作者完\n全相反。\nB) 以偏概全\n原文确实承认有代价（unforeseen and unintended consequences），但作者的核心\n立场不是\"代价不可避免\"，而是\"需要监管\"。把局部当作主旨，落点偏移。\nC) ✓ 正确\nthoughtful regulation is necessary for a better future ↔\nTechnological innovation should be properly regulated\nCentral control and collective organisation can produce smoother and fairer outcomes ↔\nTechnological innovation should be properly regulated\n预判要的\"技术需妥善监管\"，C 把 thoughtful regulation 直译为 properly\nregulated，又覆盖了 central control 这条中段证据，是全文主线的最大公约数。\nD) 以偏概全\n硅谷文化只在 Silicon Valley has to acknowledge the costs of the intoxicating hurry\n一句被点到，作为例子；全文主旨并非反对硅谷文化本身。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "主旨题"]}]});
